For ionic crystals, if we neglect the damping effect (i.e., take the damping term as \(r = 0\)), \(\varepsilon(\omega)\) becomes a real number.

\[
\varepsilon(\omega) = \varepsilon(\infty) + \frac{[\varepsilon(0) - \varepsilon(\infty)] \omega_{\mathrm{T}}^{2}}{\omega_{\mathrm{T}}^{2} - \omega^{2}}
\]

From the given conditions, when \(\omega = \omega_{0} = \frac{2 \pi c}{\lambda_{0}} = 6.15 \times 10^{13} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}\), the reflectance \(R = 0\), implying \(\varepsilon(\omega) = 1\), yielding

\[
\begin{gathered}
1 = \varepsilon(\infty) + \frac{[\varepsilon(0) - \varepsilon(\infty)] \omega_{\mathrm{T}}^{2}}{\omega_{\mathrm{T}}^{2} - \omega_{0}^{2}} \\
\omega_{\mathrm{T}} = \left[\frac{\varepsilon(\infty) - 1}{\varepsilon(0) - 1}\right]^{1 / 2} \omega_{0} = 0.505 \omega_{0} = 3.11 \times 10^{13} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{gathered}
\]

According to the LST relation:

\[
\frac{\omega_{\mathrm{L}}^{2}}{\omega_{\mathrm{T}}^{2}} = \frac{\varepsilon(0)}{\varepsilon(\infty)}
\]

Therefore,

\[
\omega_{\mathrm{L}} = \left[\frac{\varepsilon(0)}{\varepsilon(\infty)}\right]^{1 / 2} \omega_{\mathrm{T}} = 0.817 \omega_{0} = 5.03 \times 10^{13} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\]

For a relativistic particle with a current of 10 mA moving at the speed of light within a circle of radius 10 m, the effective charge per round of motion is $q = 2 \pi r i / c$, corresponding to the electron and positron flow as

$$
N = \frac{q}{e} = \frac{2 \pi r i}{e c} = \frac{2 \pi \times 10 \times 0.01}{3 \times 10^{8} \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19}} = 1.31 \times 10^{10} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
$$

Under the given conditions, the positron and electron beams collide $n = 2$ times per cycle. The number of cycles per second that the electrons execute in the ring is $f = c / 2 \pi r$, and the cross-sectional area of the beam is $A = 0.1 \, \mathrm{cm}^{2}$. The reaction rate is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
R & = \sigma \left(\frac{N}{A}\right)^{2} \times A \times n \times f \approx 1.5 \times 10^{-30} \times \left(\frac{1.31 \times 10^{10}}{0.1}\right)^{2} \\
& \quad \times \frac{0.1 \times 3 \times 10^{10} \times 2}{2 \pi \times 10} \\
& \approx 2.46 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1} \approx 90 \, \mathrm{h}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus, there are approximately 90 instances of the $\mathrm{e}^{+} \mathrm{e}^{-} \rightarrow \pi^{+} \pi^{-} \pi^{0}$ reaction per hour.

