Taking the center of the loop as the origin, let the position vector of a point in space be \(\boldsymbol{r}(r, \theta, \varphi)\). When \( r \rightarrow \infty \), the vector potential at this point is

\[
\boldsymbol{A}(\boldsymbol{r}, t) = \frac{i k \mu_{0} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k r}}{4 \pi r} \boldsymbol{n} \times \boldsymbol{m}
\]

where \(\boldsymbol{n} = \boldsymbol{e}_{\mathrm{r}}, k = \frac{\omega}{c} \), taking the complex form of the magnetic dipole moment

\[
\boldsymbol{m} = \pi a^{2} i_{0} \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \omega t} \boldsymbol{e}_{z}
\]

The real value is understood to be its real part, thus the formula for the vector potential is

\[
\boldsymbol{A}(r, t) = -i \frac{\mu_{0} \omega i_{0} a^{2} \sin \theta}{4 c r} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(k r-\omega t)} \boldsymbol{e}_{\varphi}
\]

The corresponding radiated electromagnetic field is

\[
\begin{aligned}
& \boldsymbol{B} = \nabla \times \boldsymbol{A} = i k \boldsymbol{n} \times \boldsymbol{A} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{2} i_{0} a^{2} \sin \theta}{4 c^{2} r} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(k r-\omega t)} \boldsymbol{e}_{\theta} \\
& \boldsymbol{E} = c \boldsymbol{B} \times \boldsymbol{n} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{2} i_{0} a^{2} \sin \theta}{4 c r} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(k r-\omega t)} \boldsymbol{e}_{\varphi}
\end{aligned}
\]

The average energy flux density is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\overline{\boldsymbol{S}} & = \frac{1}{2} \operatorname{Re}\left\{\boldsymbol{E}^{*} \times \boldsymbol{H}\right\} = \frac{c}{2 \mu_{0}} \operatorname{Re}\left\{\left(\boldsymbol{B}^{*} \times \boldsymbol{n}\right) \times \boldsymbol{B}\right\} \\
& = \frac{c}{2 \mu_{0}}|\boldsymbol{B}|^{2} \boldsymbol{n} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{4} i_{0}^{2} a^{4}}{32 c^{3} r^{2}} \sin^{2} \theta \boldsymbol{n}
\end{aligned}
\]

Let \( P \) be the average radiated power, the radiation angle distribution is

\[
\frac{\mathrm{d} P}{\mathrm{~d} \Omega} = S r^{2} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{4} a^{4} i_{0}^{2}}{32 c^{3}} \sin^{2} \theta
\]

The root mean square radius of the nucleus is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left\langle r^{2} \right\rangle & = \langle \Psi| r^{2} |\Psi \rangle_{r < b} + \langle \Psi| r^{2} |\Psi \rangle_{r > b} \\
& = \frac{b^{2}}{1 + \frac{1}{k_{1}b}} \left[\left(\frac{1}{3} + \frac{4}{\pi^{2}}\right) + \frac{1}{k_{1}b} + \frac{1}{(k_{1}b)^{2}} + \frac{1}{2(k_{1}b)^{3}}\right] \\
& \approx \frac{b^{2}}{2} \left( \frac{1}{3} + \frac{4}{\pi^{2}} + 2.5 \right) = 5.8 \text{ fm}^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The total mechanical energy in the region between adjacent particle surfaces vibrating in phase is:

$$
\begin{aligned}
E &= \bar{\varepsilon} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \pi d^{2} \lambda = \bar{\varepsilon} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \pi d^{2} \frac{v}{\nu} \\
&= 3.0 \times 10^{-5} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \pi (0.14)^{2} \cdot \frac{300}{300} = 4.62 \times 10^{-7} \, \mathrm{J}
\end{aligned}
$$

Once the sample is removed from the reactor, $\mathrm{Sb}$ is no longer produced, and its number gradually decreases. At this point:

$$
\begin{aligned}
N_{1}(t)= & N_{1}(T) \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{1} t}, \\
N_{2}(t)= & \frac{\lambda_{1}}{\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}} N_{1}(T) \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{1} t}+\left[N_{2}(T)-\frac{\lambda_{1} N_{1}(T)}{\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}}\right] \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{2} t}, \\
N_{3}(t)= & \frac{\lambda_{1} \lambda_{2} N_{1}(T)}{\left(\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}\right)\left(\lambda_{3}-\lambda_{1}\right)} \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{1} t}+\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\lambda_{3}-\lambda_{2}}\left[N_{2}(T)-\frac{\lambda_{1} N_{1}(T)}{\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}}\right] \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{2} t} \\
& +\left[N_{3}(T)-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\lambda_{3}-\lambda_{2}} N_{2}(T)-\frac{\lambda_{1} \lambda_{2} N_{1}(T)}{\left(\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{3}\right)\left(\lambda_{3}-\lambda_{2}\right)}\right] \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{3} t}
\end{aligned}
$$

For $t=12$ hours, where $t \gg \tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$, we have:

$$
\begin{aligned}
N_{3}(12 \mathrm{~h}) & \approx\left[N_{3}(T)-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\lambda_{3}-\lambda_{2}} N_{2}(T)+\frac{\lambda_{1} \lambda_{2} N_{1}(T)}{\left(\lambda_{1}-\lambda_{3}\right)\left(\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{3}\right)}\right] \mathrm{e}^{-\lambda_{3} t} \\
& \approx 10^{10} \times(2.77+8.78+3.56) \exp \left(-8.75 \times 10^{-6} \times 12 \times 3600\right) \\
& \approx 1.04 \times 10^{11}
\end{aligned}
$$

Given a separation efficiency of $0.75$, the number of separated iodine ($\mathrm{I}$) atoms is:

$$
N=0.75 \times 1.04 \times 10^{11}=7.8 \times 10^{10}
$$

Let the escape velocity be $v$. The condition for escape is given by

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{1}{2} m v^{2} - \frac{G M_{\mathrm{m}} m}{R_{\mathrm{m}}} = 0 \\
v = \sqrt{\frac{2 G M_{\mathrm{m}}}{R_{\mathrm{m}}}} = \sqrt{2 g_{\mathrm{m}} R_{\mathrm{m}}} = \sqrt{2 \times 1.62 \times 1.74 \times 10^{6}} \\
= 2.37 \times 10^{3} \mathrm{~m/s}
\end{gathered}
$$

**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the reference frame where particle 1 (the uncollided particle) is at rest, the other particle adopts the reduced mass $\mu = \frac{2}{3}m$. According to the conservation of mechanical energy:

\[
\frac{1}{2}\mu\left(\dot{r}^{2}+r^{2}\dot{\varphi}^{2}\right)+\frac{1}{2}k(r-a)^{2}=\frac{1}{2}\mu v^{2} = \frac{1}{8}\mu V^{2} \tag{1}
\]

According to the conservation of angular momentum with respect to particle 1 (considered as the origin):

\[
\mu r^{2} \dot{\varphi} = \mu v a = \frac{1}{2}\mu a V \tag{2}
\]

When $r=r_{\text{max}}=3a$, $\dot{r}=0$, substitute into equations (1) and (2):

\[
\begin{gathered}
9\mu a^{2} \dot{\varphi}^{2} + 4ka^{2} = \frac{1}{4}\mu V^{2} \tag{3}\\
9a \dot{\varphi} = \frac{1}{2} V \tag{4}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solve equations (3) and (4) for $V$, and substitute $\mu=\frac{2}{3}m$ to obtain:

\[
V = \sqrt{\frac{18ka^{2}}{\mu}} = 3a\sqrt{\frac{3k}{m}}
\]

Using the Gell-Mann–Nishijima formula to determine the $I_{3}$ of $\Xi^{-}$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& I_{3} = Q - \frac{1}{2}(B+S) = (-1) - \frac{(1 - 2)}{2} = -\frac{1}{2} \\
\end{aligned}
$$

The change in wavelength due to Compton scattering is independent of the wavelength of the incident light. The change in wavelength is maximum when the scattering angle $\theta=180^{\circ}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta \lambda_{\mathrm{m}} & =\frac{h}{m_{\mathrm{e}} c}\left(1-\cos 180^{\circ}\right)=\frac{2 h}{m_{\mathrm{e}} c} \\
& =\frac{2 \times 1.24 \, \text{keV} \cdot \text{nm}}{0.511 \, \text{MeV}}=0.00485 \, \text{nm}
\end{aligned}
$$

The energy difference between the incident photon and the scattered photon, which is the maximum energy of the recoiling electron, is

$$
E_{\mathrm{k}}=\Delta E_{\nu}=\frac{h c}{\lambda}-\frac{h c}{\lambda^{\prime}}=\frac{h c}{\lambda} \cdot \frac{\Delta \lambda}{(\lambda+\Delta \lambda)}
$$

For the X-ray with $\lambda_{1}=0.050 \, \text{nm}$,

$$
E_{\mathrm{k}_{1}}=\frac{h c}{\lambda_{1}} \cdot \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\left(\lambda_{1}+\Delta \lambda\right)}=\frac{1.24 \, \text{keV} \cdot \text{nm}}{0.050 \, \text{nm}} \cdot \frac{0.00485 \, \text{nm}}{(0.050 + 0.00485) \, \text{nm}}=2.2 \, \text{keV}
$$

problem:
Given the decay of ${}^{17} \mathrm{N}$ to an excited state of ${}^{17} \mathrm{O}^*$, the maximum emitted $\beta$ energy is $3.72 \mathrm{MeV}$. This excited state of ${}^{17} \mathrm{O}^*$ decays by emitting a neutron. Using the following mass difference data and experimental data, calculate the energy level of the emitted neutron.

$$
\begin{gathered}
m\left({}^{17} \mathrm{N}\right)-m\left({}^{17} \mathrm{O}\right)=8.8 \mathrm{MeV} / c^{2} \\
m(\mathrm{n})-m\left({}^{1} \mathrm{H}\right)=0.78 \mathrm{MeV} / c^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

The energy levels of the excited states of ${}^{16} \mathrm{O}$ are $6.05 \mathrm{MeV}$, $6.13 \mathrm{MeV}$, $6.97 \mathrm{MeV}$, $7.1 \mathrm{MeV}$, and higher levels.

solution:
When considering the motion of the nucleus, the reduced mass is used in place of the electron mass $m$ in the expression for the Rydberg constant. The reduced masses for hydrogen and deuterium atoms are:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mu_{\mathrm{H}} & =\frac{m M_{\mathrm{H}}}{m+M_{\mathrm{H}}} \\
\mu_{\mathrm{D}} & =\frac{m M_{\mathrm{D}}}{m+M_{\mathrm{D}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus, the wavelengths of $\mathrm{H}_{\alpha}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{\alpha}$ are given by:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \lambda_{\mathrm{H}}=\left[\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{H}} e^{4}}{8 \varepsilon_{0}^{2} h^{3} c}\left(\frac{1}{2^{2}}-\frac{1}{3^{2}}\right)\right]^{-1} \\
& \lambda_{\mathrm{D}}=\left[\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{D}} e^{4}}{8 \varepsilon_{0}^{2} h^{3} c}\left(\frac{1}{2^{2}}-\frac{1}{3^{2}}\right)\right]^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

Dividing the two equations, we obtain:

$$
\frac{\lambda_{\mathrm{H}}}{\lambda_{\mathrm{D}}}=\frac{\mu_{\mathrm{D}}}{\mu_{\mathrm{H}}}=\frac{1+\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{H}}}}{1+\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{D}}}}
$$

Thus:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{D}}}-\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{H}}}=\frac{\lambda_{\mathrm{D}}-\lambda_{\mathrm{H}}}{\lambda_{\mathrm{H}}} \\
\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{D}}}=\frac{m}{M_{\mathrm{H}}}-\frac{\lambda_{\mathrm{H}}-\lambda_{\mathrm{D}}}{\lambda_{\mathrm{H}}}=\frac{1}{1836}-\frac{656.280-656.101}{656.280}=2.79 \times 10^{-4} \\
M_{\mathrm{D}}=3678 m \\
\frac{M_{\mathrm{D}}}{M_{\mathrm{H}}}=\frac{3678 m}{1836 m}=2.003 \approx 2
\end{gathered}
$$

Therefore, the mass of deuterium is twice the mass of hydrogen, and historically, it was discovered through measuring the wavelengths of $\mathrm{H}_{\alpha}$ and $\mathrm{D}_{\alpha}$ spectral lines.

The weak interaction reaction rate is given by

$$
\Gamma=2 \pi G_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}|M|^{2} \frac{\mathrm{d} N}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}
$$

where $\frac{\mathrm{d} N}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}$ represents the number of final states per unit energy interval, $M$ is the transition matrix element, and $G_{F}$ is the weak interaction coupling constant.  
The two decay modes of the $\pi^{-}$ particle are:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \pi^{-} \rightarrow \mu^{-}+\bar{\nu}_{\mu} \\
& \pi^{-} \rightarrow \mathrm{e}^{-}+\bar{\nu}_{e}
\end{aligned}
$$

Assuming a four-fermion interaction with a nucleon-antinucleon intermediate state. Considering parity, angular momentum, etc., according to the V-A theory, this coupling is axial vector coupling (A). For A coupling, $M^{2} \approx 1-\beta$, where $\beta$ is the speed of the charged lepton. The phase space factor is

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d} N}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}=B p^{2} \cdot \frac{\mathrm{d} p}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}
$$

where $B$ is a constant, and $p$ is the momentum of the charged lepton in the rest frame of the $\pi$ meson. The total energy of the system is

$$
\begin{aligned}
& E_{0}=m_{\pi}=p+\sqrt{p^{2}+m^{2}} \\
& p=\frac{m_{\pi}^{2}-m^{2}}{2 m_{\pi}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Here, $m$ is the rest mass of the lepton, and the neutrino mass is zero. Differentiating the above equation yields

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{d} E_{0}=\mathrm{d} p+\frac{p \mathrm{d} p}{\sqrt{p^{2}+m^{2}}}=\frac{E_{0} \mathrm{d} p}{E_{0}-p}=\frac{2 m_{\pi}^{2}}{m_{\pi}^{2}+m^{2}} \mathrm{d} p \\
& \frac{\mathrm{d} p}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}=\frac{m_{\pi}^{2}+m^{2}}{2 m_{\pi}^{2}} \\
& \beta=\frac{p}{\sqrt{p^{2}+m^{2}}}=\frac{p}{m_{\pi}-p} \\
& 1-\beta=1-\frac{p}{m_{\pi}-p}=\frac{m_{\pi}-2 p}{m_{\pi}-p}=\frac{2 m_{\pi}^{2}-2\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m^{2}\right)}{2 m_{\pi}^{2}-\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m^{2}\right)}=\frac{2 m^{2}}{m_{\pi}^{2}+m^{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

For A coupling, the decay rate $\Gamma$ is

$$
\Gamma \propto(1-\beta) p^{2} \frac{\mathrm{d} p}{\mathrm{d} E_{0}}=\frac{1}{4}\left(\frac{m}{m_{\pi}}\right)^{2}\left(\frac{m_{\pi}^{2}-m^{2}}{m_{\pi}}\right)^{2}
$$

The ratio is

$$
\begin{aligned}
R & =\frac{\Gamma\left(\pi^{-} \rightarrow \mu^{-} \bar{\nu}_{\mu}\right)}{\Gamma\left(\pi^{-} \rightarrow \mathrm{e}^{-} \bar{\nu}_{e}\right)}=\frac{\frac{m_{\mu}^{2}}{4}\left(1-\frac{m_{\mu}^{2}}{m_{\pi}^{2}}\right)^{2}}{\frac{m_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}{4}\left(1-\frac{m_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}}{m_{\pi}^{2}}\right)^{2}} \\
& =\frac{m_{\mu}^{2}\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m_{\mu}^{2}\right)^{2}}{m_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m_{\mathrm{e}}^{2}\right)^{2}}=8.13 \times 10^{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

problem:
The half-life of \(^{239}\mathrm{Pu}\) can be determined by the following method: Place a sphere of \(^{239}\mathrm{Pu}\) with a mass of 120.1 g in liquid nitrogen, with the liquid nitrogen volume sufficient to stop all \(\alpha\) particles. Measure the evaporation rate of the liquid nitrogen, which corresponds to a power of 0.231 W. Calculate the energy released per \(\alpha\) decay of \(^{239}\mathrm{Pu}\). The energy of the \(\alpha\) particle is 5.1 MeV. Conversion factors are

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 \mathrm{MeV} & = 1.60206 \times 10^{-13} \mathrm{~J} \\
1 \mathrm{u} & = 1.66 \times 10^{-24} \mathrm{~g}
\end{aligned}
\]

solution:
At a temperature $T \approx 0 \ \mathrm{K}$, the Fermi energy is given by

\begin{equation*}
\varepsilon_{\mathrm{F}} = \frac{h^{2}}{2m}\left(\frac{3n}{8 \pi}\right)^{2/3} \tag{1}
\end{equation*}

where $n$ is the number density of conduction electrons. From equation (1), we can derive the expression for the number density of conduction electrons in Pb as

\begin{equation*}
n_{e} = \frac{8 \pi}{3}\left(\frac{2 m \varepsilon_{\mathrm{F}}}{h^{2}}\right)^{3/2} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

Substituting the electron mass $m = 9.11 \times 10^{-31} \ \mathrm{kg}$, Planck's constant $h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \ \mathrm{J \cdot s}$, Fermi energy $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{F}} = 9.4 \ \mathrm{eV}$, and $1 \ \mathrm{eV} = 1.60 \times 10^{-19} \ \mathrm{J}$ into equation (2), we find the number density of conduction electrons in Pb

\begin{equation*}
n_{e} = 1.30 \times 10^{29} \ \mathrm{m}^{-3} = 1.30 \times 10^{23} \ \mathrm{cm}^{-3} \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

