Let the area of the two-dimensional close-packed structure be \(S\), and the number of atoms be \(N\). Each atom contributes one free electron, thus we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
2 \cdot \frac{S}{(2 \pi)^{2}} \cdot \pi k_{\mathrm{F}}^{2} = N \\
k_{\mathrm{F}}^{2} = 2 \pi \frac{N}{S}
\end{gathered}
\]

\(\frac{N}{S}\) is the number of atoms per unit area, which can be calculated as follows. Consider an equilateral triangle with side length \(a\), and all three angles equal to \(60^{\circ}\), so its area is

\[
\frac{1}{2} \times a \times a \times \sin 60^{\circ} = \frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} a^{2}
\]

However, each atom at the vertices of the equilateral triangle only contributes \(\frac{1}{6}\) of an atom to the triangle, so one equilateral triangle contains only \(\frac{1}{2}\) of an atom

\[
\frac{N}{S} = \frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} a^{2}} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{3} a^{2}}
\]

Thus the Fermi wave vector \(k_{\mathrm{F}}^{2} = 2 \pi \cdot \frac{2}{\sqrt{3} a^{2}}\), which means

\[
k_{\mathrm{F}} = \frac{2}{a}\left(\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{3}}\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
\]

Referring to the diagram in the problem, after the emission of a single photon $h \nu^{\prime}$, the energy and momentum conservation formulas are:

$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
\frac{1}{2} mv_{1}^{2} + E = \frac{1}{2} mv_{1}^{\prime 2} + h \nu^{\prime} \\
mv_{1} = mv_{1}^{\prime} \cos \varphi + \frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{c} \cos \theta \\
0 = mv_{1}^{\prime} \sin \varphi - \frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{c} \sin \theta
\end{array}
\right.
$$

Since the changes $v_{1}^{\prime} = v_{1} + \Delta v^{\prime}$ and $\nu^{\prime} = \nu + \Delta \nu^{\prime}$ are very small, when $\theta = \frac{\pi}{2}$, the deflection angle $\varphi$ reaches its maximum, which is:

$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
mv_{1} = mv_{1}^{\prime} \cos \varphi \\
\frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{c} = mv_{1}^{\prime} \sin \varphi
\end{array}
\right.
$$

From this, we derive:

$$
\tan \varphi_{\text{max}} = \frac{h \nu^{\prime}}{mv_{1}c}
$$

Substituting $\nu^{\prime} \approx \nu$ gives:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\tan \varphi_{\text{max}} = \frac{E}{mv_{1}c} \\
\varphi_{\text{max}} = \arctan \frac{E}{mv_{1}c} \approx \arctan \frac{E}{mv_{0}c} = 5 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{rad}
\end{gathered}
$$

