solution:
By conservation of momentum, the momentum and its rate of change for the light moving backward is equal to the momentum and its rate of change for the rocket moving forward:

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d} p}{\mathrm{d} t}=m \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t}
$$

And

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d} p}{\mathrm{d} t}=\frac{1}{c} \frac{\mathrm{d} E}{\mathrm{d} t}=m \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t}
$$

Acceleration is given by

$$
\begin{gathered}
a=\frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t}=\frac{1}{mc} \frac{\mathrm{d} E}{\mathrm{d} t} \\
t=\frac{v_{t}}{a}=\frac{v_{t} mc}{\mathrm{d} E / \mathrm{d} t}=3 \times 10^{8} \mathrm{~s} \approx 10 \mathrm{years}
\end{gathered}
$$

It takes approximately 10 years to accelerate the small rocket to $1 \mathrm{~m/s}$.

$$
l=\frac{1}{\sqrt{2} \sigma} \cdot \frac{kT}{p} \approx \frac{1}{\pi \cdot (10^{-13})^{2}} \cdot \frac{1.38 \times 10^{-16} \times 300}{10^{6}} \approx 10^{6} \mathrm{~cm}
$$

**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

problem:
An atom has four lowest energy levels:

\[ E_{0}=-14.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{1}=-9.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{2}=-7.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{3}=-5.5 \, \mathrm{eV} \]

The transition rate \(A_{ij}\) (Einstein coefficient) for the transition \(i \rightarrow j\) is given by:

\[
\begin{aligned}
& A_{10} = 3.0 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad A_{20} = 1.2 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1} \\
& A_{21} = 8.0 \times 10^{7} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Consider a container containing a large number of atoms in the energy level \(E_{2}\). Find the ratio of the energy emitted per unit time for the transition \(E_{2} \rightarrow E_{0}\) to the transition \(E_{2} \rightarrow E_{1}\).

solution:
The spectral terms for the lithium atom are

$$
T_{3 \mathrm{s}}=\frac{R}{\left(3-\Delta_{\mathrm{s}}\right)^{2}}, \quad T_{2 \mathrm{p}}=\frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{p}}\right)^{2}}
$$

The wavenumber of the spectral line for the transition from the 3s state to the 2p state is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{\nu}_{3 \mathrm{s} \rightarrow 2 \mathrm{p}} & = T_{2 \mathrm{p}} - T_{3 \mathrm{s}} = \frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{p}}\right)^{2}} - \frac{R}{\left(3-\Delta_{\mathrm{s}}\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{13.6 \times 10^{10}}{12400 \times (2-0.05)^{2}} - \frac{13.6 \times 10^{10}}{12400 \times (3-0.40)^{2}} \\
& = 1.26 \times 10^{6} \, \mathrm{m}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

In the rest frame of $\pi^{+}$, the laws of conservation of energy and momentum require

$$
E_{\mu}^{*}+E_{\nu}^{*}=m_{\pi}, \quad p_{\mu}^{*}=p_{\nu}^{*}
$$

Simultaneously solving the two equations gives

$$
m_{\mu}^{2}+p_{\nu}^{* 2}=E_{\nu}^{* 2}+m_{\pi}^{2}-2 m_{\pi} E_{\nu}^{*}
$$

Assuming the neutrino mass is $0, E_{\nu}^{*}=p_{\nu}^{*}$, the equation becomes

$$
\begin{aligned}
E_{\nu}^{*} & =\frac{m_{\pi}^{2}-m_{\mu}^{2}}{2 m_{\pi}} \\
& =\frac{140^{2}-106^{2}}{2 \times 140}=30\, \text{MeV}
\end{aligned}
$$

(4) The time evolution of the wave function of the neutrino mass eigenstates can be written as

\[
\begin{aligned}
& \left|\nu_{1}(t)\right\rangle=\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{1} t} \\
& \left|\nu_{2}(t)\right\rangle=\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{2} t}
\end{aligned}
\]

We express the wave functions of type \(a\) and type \(b\) neutrinos in terms of \(\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle\) and \(\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle\), as follows:

\[
\begin{aligned}
& \left|\nu_{\mathrm{a}}\right\rangle=\cos \theta\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle+\sin \theta\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle \\
& \left|\nu_{\mathrm{b}}\right\rangle=-\sin \theta\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle+\cos \theta\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle
\end{aligned}
\]

At time \(t\), the wave functions of type \(a\) and type \(b\) neutrinos are:

\[
\begin{aligned}
& \left|\nu_{\mathrm{a}}(t)\right\rangle=\cos \theta\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{1} t}+\sin \theta\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{2} t} \\
& \left|\nu_{\mathrm{b}}(t)\right\rangle=-\sin \theta\left|\nu_{1}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{1} t}+\cos \theta\left|\nu_{2}\right\rangle \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} E_{2} t}
\end{aligned}
\]

At time \(t = 0\), a pure beam of type \(a\) neutrinos \(\left|\nu_{\mathrm{a}}\right\rangle\) exists, and at time \(t\), the probabilities of observing type \(a\) and type \(b\) neutrinos are respectively:

\[
\begin{aligned}
p_{\mathrm{a}}(t) & =\left|\left\langle\nu_{\mathrm{a}} \mid \nu_{\mathrm{a}}(t)\right\rangle\right|^{2}=\cos ^{4} \theta+\sin ^{4} \theta+2 \cos ^{2} \theta \sin ^{2} \theta \cos (\Delta E t) \\
& =\left(\cos ^{2} \theta+\sin ^{2} \theta\right)-4 \cos ^{2} \theta \sin ^{2} \theta \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{1}{2} \Delta E t\right) \\
& =1-\left(\sin ^{2} 2 \theta\right) \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{1}{2} \Delta E t\right) \\
p_{\mathrm{b}}(t) & =1-p_{\mathrm{a}}(t)=\sin ^{2} 2 \theta \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{1}{2} \Delta E t\right)
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(\Delta E=E_{1}-E_{2}\). For further analysis, we assume that neutrinos with different mass eigenstates have the same momentum but different energies, and thus different speeds. The coherence length is much greater than the distance to the supernova. By expressing energy as a function of mass, we have:

\[
\begin{aligned}
& E=\sqrt{p^{2}+m^{2}} \approx p+\frac{m^{2}}{2 p} \\
& \Delta E \approx \frac{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}{2 p} \approx \frac{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}{2 \bar{E}_{\nu}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Since the neutrino mass is very small, \(\bar{E}_{\nu}\) is used in place of \(p\) in the equation. Substituting into the probability equations, we obtain:

\[
\begin{gathered}
p_{\mathrm{a}}(t)=1-\sin ^{2} 2 \theta \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}{4 \bar{E}_{\nu}} l\right) \\
p_{\mathrm{b}}(t)=\sin ^{2} 2 \theta \sin ^{2}\left(\frac{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}{4 \bar{E}_{\nu}} l\right)
\end{gathered}
\]

where \(l\) is the flight distance. The oscillation length \(l_{0}\) is defined as:

\[
l_{0}=\frac{4 \bar{E}_{\nu}}{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}
\]

Assuming the distance from Earth to the supernova \(D\) is much smaller than the oscillation length \(l_{0}\), and if no significant neutrino oscillation is detected on Earth, the upper limit on the neutrino mass squared difference is:

\[
\begin{gathered}
\frac{m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}}{4 \bar{E}_{\nu}} l_{0}=\pi \\
\Delta m^{2}=m_{2}^{2}-m_{1}^{2}<\frac{4 \pi \bar{E}_{\nu}}{D}
\end{gathered}
\]

Substituting the specific values, we finally obtain:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta m^{2}<\frac{4 \pi \bar{E}_{\nu}}{D} & =\frac{4 \pi \times 10^{7}}{3 \times 10^{10} \times 3.16 \times 10^{7} \times 1.7 \times 10^{5}} \\
& \approx 0.8 \times 10^{-15}\left(\mathrm{eV}^{2}\right)
\end{aligned}
\]

Utilizing \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \approx 2 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{MeV} = 2 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{eV}\) in the equation.

During this process, the change in entropy of the water is $\Delta S_{1}^{\prime} = 0.312 \mathrm{kcal}/\mathrm{K}$. The change in entropy of the heat reservoir is
$$
\begin{gathered}
\Delta S_{2}^{\prime} = -\frac{1 \times (50-0)}{273+50} - \frac{1 \times (100-50)}{273+100} = -0.289 \mathrm{kcal}/\mathrm{K} \\
\Delta S^{\prime} = \Delta S_{1}^{\prime} + \Delta S_{2}^{\prime} = 0.023 \mathrm{kcal}/\mathrm{K}
\end{gathered}
$$

The external force acting on the cloud is the electrostatic force between the image charges and the charges in the cloud

$$
\begin{aligned}
F & = \frac{q^{2}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \left[-\frac{1}{(2d)^{2}} + \frac{1}{(3d)^{2}} + \frac{1}{(3d)^{2}} - \frac{1}{(4d)^{2}}\right] \\
& = \frac{q^{2}}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0} d^{2}} \left[\frac{2}{9} - \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{16}\right] = -4.05 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{N}
\end{aligned}
$$

This force is an attractive force, as shown in Figure 1.93 in the original problem.

The stiffness \( k \) of the elastic rope is the elastic modulus divided by the original length, i.e., \( k = \frac{\lambda}{a} \).

Let the speed of the particle when the rope is stretched to the maximum length be \( v_{1} \), perpendicular to the rope. Using the conservation of mechanical energy and the conservation of angular momentum with respect to the fixed point \( O \), we can establish the following relationships:

\[
\begin{gathered}
\frac{1}{2} m v_{1}^{2} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{\lambda}{a}(3a-a)^{2} = \frac{1}{2} m v^{2} \\
3a v_{1} = av \\
v = \sqrt{\frac{9\lambda a}{2m}}
\end{gathered}
\]

Thus, the initial velocity of the particle \( v \) is determined to be \( v = \sqrt{\frac{9\lambda a}{2m}} \) using the above relations.

If the photon undergoes elastic backscattering, the momenta of the photon and the particle after scattering are $-p_{1}$ and $p_{2}$, respectively. From the conservation of energy and momentum during the scattering process, we have:

$$
\begin{gathered}
p_{1} c+\sqrt{p_{2}^{2} c^{2}+m^{2} c^{4}}=p c+m c^{2} \\
-p_{1}+p_{2}=p
\end{gathered}
$$

By eliminating $p_{2}$, we obtain

$$
\begin{gathered}
\left(p-p_{1}\right)^{2}+2\left(p-p_{1}\right) m c=\left(p+p_{1}\right)^{2} \\
p_{1}=\frac{m c p}{2 p+m c}
\end{gathered}
$$

This is the magnitude of the momentum of the photon after backscattering in the laboratory frame.

problem:
Calculate the first 10 Fourier coefficients of a black and white grating: \( t_0, \tilde{t}_1, \cdots, \tilde{t}_9 \), where \( a/d = 1/2 \). The transmittance function of the black and white grating is given by:

\[ 
t(x) = 
\begin{cases} 
1, & |x| < \frac{a}{2} \\ 
0, & \frac{a}{2} < |x| < \frac{d}{2}
\end{cases} 
\]

The expression for the Fourier coefficient \( \tilde{t}_n \) is given by:

\[
\tilde{t}_n = \frac{a}{d} \frac{\sin (n \pi a / d)}{n \pi a / d}
\]

where \( a/d = 1/2 \).

solution:
When \( \frac{a}{d} = \frac{1}{2} \), we obtain:

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
t_0 = \frac{1}{2}, & \tilde{t}_1 = \frac{1}{\pi} \sin \frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{1}{\pi} \\
\tilde{t}_2 = \frac{1}{2 \pi} \sin \frac{2 \pi}{2} = 0, & \tilde{t}_3 = \frac{1}{3 \pi} \sin \frac{3 \pi}{2} = -\frac{1}{3 \pi} \\
\tilde{t}_4 = \frac{1}{4 \pi} \sin \frac{4 \pi}{2} = 0, & \tilde{t}_5 = \frac{1}{5 \pi} \sin \frac{5 \pi}{2} = \frac{1}{5 \pi} \\
\tilde{t}_6 = \frac{1}{6 \pi} \sin \frac{6 \pi}{2} = 0, & \tilde{t}_7 = \frac{1}{7 \pi} \sin \frac{7 \pi}{2} = -\frac{1}{7 \pi} \\
\tilde{t}_8 = \frac{1}{8 \pi} \sin \frac{8 \pi}{2} = 0, & \tilde{t}_9 = \frac{1}{9 \pi} \sin \frac{9 \pi}{2} = \frac{1}{9 \pi}
\end{array}
\]

**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

In the probability of spontaneous transition for an excited state atom,

$$
\begin{gathered}
P \propto \frac{e^{2} \omega^{3}}{\hbar c^{3}} R^{2} \\
\omega \propto \frac{m_{\mu}\left(Z e^{2}\right)^{2}}{\hbar^{3}}, \quad R \propto \frac{\hbar^{2}}{m_{\mu} Z e^{2}}
\end{gathered}
$$

Thus,

$$
P \propto \frac{m_{\mu}\left(Z e^{2}\right)^{4}}{\hbar^{5}}
$$

The average lifetime $\tau = \frac{1}{P} = \frac{1}{m_{\mu}\left(Z e^{2}\right)^{4}}$. Given that the average lifetime of a hydrogen atom in the 3d state is $\tau_{0} = 1.6 \times 10^{-8} \ \mathrm{s}$, the average lifetime of the atom in the 3d state is

$$
\tau = \frac{m_{\mathrm{e}}}{m_{\mu}} \cdot \frac{1}{Z^{4}} \tau_{0} = 2.7 \times 10^{-15} \ \mathrm{s}
$$

