**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

$$
\begin{aligned}
& P = \frac{I_{0}}{S c} = \frac{3.0 \times 10^{13}}{3 \times 10^{8} \times 10^{-7}} = 1 \times 10^{12} \, (\mathrm{N} / \mathrm{m}^{2})
\end{aligned}
$$

problem:
The total scattering and absorption cross-sections for nitrogen and oxygen atoms for neutrons with an energy of 25 MeV are given as follows:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccl}
 & $\sigma_{\mathrm{t}}$ & $\sigma_{\mathrm{a}}$ &  \\
Nitrogen atom: & 11.5 & 1.8 & (units: $10^{-28} \mathrm{~m}^{2}$) \\
Oxygen atom: & 4.2 & 0.0 &  \\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Given that the attenuation coefficient $\alpha = 0.057 \mathrm{~m}^{-1}$, estimate the attenuation of a neutron beam after traveling 1 m in dry air at a temperature of $20^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$.

A particle with mass \( m \) experiences forced oscillation under the combined action of a restoring force \(-kx\), a damping force \(-\gamma \dot{x}\), and a periodic driving force

\[
F(t) = 
\begin{cases} 
A \sin \omega t & 0 < t \leq \frac{\pi}{\omega} \\ 
0 & \frac{\pi}{\omega} < t \leq \frac{2\pi}{\omega} 
\end{cases}
\]

Determine the fourth term \( a_4 \) of the function \( f(t) \) in the steady-state kinematic equation using the first four terms of the Fourier series.

solution:
**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Ignoring the \(\delta\) potential

\[
\begin{aligned}
V\left(x_{1}, x_{2}\right) &= \begin{cases} 
0, & 0 \leqslant x_{1}, x_{2} \leqslant L \\
\infty, & \text{elsewhere}
\end{cases} \\
H_{0} &= -\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\mathrm{d} x_{1}^{2}} - \frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}}{\mathrm{d} x_{2}^{2}} + V\left(x_{1}, x_{2}\right)
\end{aligned}
\]

Applying the results of an infinite potential well,

\[
\begin{aligned}
\psi_{n l}\left(x_{1}, x_{2}\right) &= \frac{2}{L} \sin \left(\frac{n \pi}{L} x_{1}\right) \sin \left(\frac{l \pi}{L} x_{2}\right) \\
E_{n, l} &= \frac{\hbar^{2} \pi^{2}}{2mL^{2}}\left(n^{2}+l^{2}\right), \quad n, l = 1, 2, \cdots
\end{aligned}
\]

For the ground state, \( n = l = 1 \),

\[
E_{11} = \frac{\hbar^{2} \pi^{2}}{m L^{2}}
\]

The energy carried by the pulse is distributed as electric field energy and magnetic field energy along the transmission line. The length of the distribution is

\[
l = v t = 2.5 \times 10^{8} \times 10^{-8} = 2.5 \, \text{m}
\]

Therefore,

\[
\begin{aligned}
W_{\mathrm{c}} &= \frac{1}{2}(C l) \cdot V^{2} = \frac{1}{2}\left(4 \times 10^{-11} \times 2.5\right) \times 15^{2} = 1.125 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{J} \\
W_{\mathrm{m}} &= \frac{1}{2}(L l) I^{2} = \frac{1}{2} \times 4 \times 10^{-7} \times 2.5 \times 0.15^{2} = 1.125 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{J}
\end{aligned}
\]

For the case of emitting an internal conversion electron, we have the following equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
E^{2} &= p^{2} c^{2}+m^{2} c^{4}=\left(T+m c^{2}\right)^{2} \\
p^{2} &= \frac{\left(T+m c^{2}\right)^{2}-m^{2} c^{4}}{c^{2}}=\frac{\left(T+2 m c^{2}\right) T}{c^{2}} \\
&= \frac{\left(E_{\gamma}-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{K}}\right)\left(E_{\gamma}-\varepsilon_{\mathrm{K}}+2 m c^{2}\right)}{c^{2}}=2 M E_{\mathrm{eR}} \\
& E_{\mathrm{eR}}=4.8 \, \text{(eV)}
\end{aligned}
$$

**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

According to the problem, the ground state hydrogen atom is excited to the \( n=5 \) state.

$$
\begin{aligned}
h \nu & = R_{\mathrm{H}} h c \left( \frac{1}{1^2} - \frac{1}{5^2} \right) \\
& = 13.6 \times \left( \frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{25} \right) = 13.06 \ \mathrm{eV}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $\chi = rR$, where $R$ is the radial wave function, then we have  
$$  
\begin{array}{ll}  
\chi_{l}^{\prime \prime}(r) + \left[k^{2} - \frac{l(l+1)}{r^{2}}\right] \chi_{l}(r) = 0, & r > a, \quad k^{2} = \frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}} \\  
\chi_{l}^{\prime \prime}(r) + \left[k^{\prime 2} - \frac{l(l+1)}{r^{2}}\right] \chi_{l}(r) = 0, & r < a \\  
k^{\prime 2} = \frac{2m(E + V_{0})}{\hbar^{2}}  
\end{array}  
$$  

Under the low-energy condition, calculate the s-wave partial wave, $l = 0$,  

$$  
\begin{array}{ll}  
\chi_{l}^{\prime \prime}(r) + k^{2} \chi_{l}(r) = 0, & r > a \\  
\chi_{l}^{\prime \prime}(r) + k^{\prime 2} \chi_{l}(r) = 0, & r < a  
\end{array}  
$$  

The solutions for the wave function are of the form  

$$  
\chi_{l}(r) =  
\begin{cases}  
A \sin \left(k^{\prime} r\right), & r < a \\  
A^{\prime} \sin \left(kr + \delta_{0}\right), & r > a  
\end{cases}  
$$  

The condition of continuity is that $\left(\ln \chi_{l}\right)^{\prime}$ is continuous at $r = a$, thus  

$$  
\begin{aligned}  
& k^{\prime} a \cot k^{\prime} a = k a \cot \left(k a + \delta_{0}\right) \\  
& \tan \left(k a + \delta_{0}\right) = \frac{k}{k^{\prime}} \tan k^{\prime} a \\  
& \delta_{0} = \arctan \left(\frac{k}{k^{\prime}} \tan k^{\prime} a \right) - k a  
\end{aligned}  
$$  

As $k \rightarrow 0, k^{\prime} \rightarrow k_{0} = \sqrt{\frac{2mV_{0}}{\hbar^{2}}}$  

$$  
\frac{k}{\tan \left(k a + \delta_{0}\right)} = \frac{k_{0}}{\tan k_{0} a}  
$$  

is a constant, so  

$$  
\begin{aligned}  
k a + \delta_{0} & \rightarrow 0 \\  
\tan \left(k a + \delta_{0}\right) & \approx k a + \delta_{0}  
\end{aligned}  
$$  

Therefore,  

$$  
\begin{aligned}  
\delta_{0} & \approx k a \left( \frac{\tan k_{0} a}{k_{0} a} - 1 \right) \\  
\sigma & \approx \frac{4 \pi}{k^{2}} \sin^{2} \delta_{0} \approx \frac{4 \pi}{k^{2}} \delta_{0}^{2} = 4 \pi a^{2} \left( \frac{\tan k_{0} a}{k_{0} a} - 1 \right)^{2}  
\end{aligned}  
$$  

When $k_{0} a \ll 1$  

$$  
\sigma \approx 4 \pi a^{2} \left[ \frac{1}{3}(k_{0} a)^{2} \right]^{2} = \frac{16 \pi a^{6} m^{2} V_{0}^{2}}{9 \hbar^{4}}  
$$

The average energy flux density is given by

$$
\begin{aligned}
\overline{\boldsymbol{S}} & = \frac{1}{\mu_{0}} \overline{\operatorname{Re} \boldsymbol{E} \times \operatorname{Re} \boldsymbol{B}} \\
& = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{6} q^{2} z_{0}^{4} \sin^{2} \theta \cos^{2} \theta}{16 \pi^{2} c^{3} r^{2}} \overline{\sin^{2}(2 \omega t)} \boldsymbol{n}
\end{aligned}
$$

And

$$
\overline{\sin^{2}(2 \omega t)} = \frac{1}{T} \int_{0}^{T} \sin^{2}(2 \omega t) \mathrm{d} t = \frac{1}{2}
$$

Thus,

$$
\overline{\boldsymbol{S}} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{6} q^{2} z_{0}^{4} \sin^{2} \theta \cos^{2} \theta}{32 \pi^{2} c^{3} r^{2}} \boldsymbol{n}
$$

The radiation power per unit solid angle is

$$
\frac{\mathrm{d} P}{\mathrm{d} \Omega} = \overline{\boldsymbol{S}} \cdot \boldsymbol{n} r^{2} = \frac{\mu_{0} \omega^{6} q^{2} z_{0}^{4} \sin^{2} \theta \cos^{2} \theta}{32 \pi^{2} c^{3}}
$$

The spectral terms for the lithium atom are given by

$$
T_{2 \mathrm{s}}=\frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{s}}\right)^{2}}, \quad T_{2 \mathrm{p}}=\frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{p}}\right)^{2}}
$$

The wavenumber of the transition from the 2p state to the 2s state is

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tilde{\nu}_{2 \mathrm{p} \rightarrow 2 \mathrm{s}} & = T_{2 \mathrm{s}} - T_{2 \mathrm{p}} = \frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{s}}\right)^{2}} - \frac{R}{\left(2-\Delta_{\mathrm{p}}\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{13.6 \times 10^{10}}{12400 \times (2-0.40)^{2}} - \frac{13.6 \times 10^{10}}{12400 \times (2-0.05)^{2}} \\
& = 1.40 \times 10^{6} \, \mathrm{m}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
$$

The center of mass of the binary star system is located at the midpoint of the line connecting the two stars. In the reference frame of the center of mass, applying Newton's second law in the radial direction for one of the stars gives:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{M v^{2}}{\frac{1}{2} L} & = G \frac{M^{2}}{L^{2}} \\
v^{2} & = \frac{G M}{2 L}
\end{aligned}
\]

The rotational period \(T\) is then given by:

\[
T = \frac{2 \pi \cdot \frac{1}{2} L}{v} = \pi L \sqrt{\frac{2 L}{G M}}
\]

