solution:
Consider a particle located at point $A$ in the figure. The component of the force exerted by the particle at point $B$ on it in the $OA$ direction is 

$$
\mu m^{2} \cdot 2r \sin^{2} \frac{\pi}{n}
$$

The components of the forces exerted by other particles on the particle at point $A$ in the $OA$ direction are $\mu m^{2} \cdot 2r \sin^{2} \frac{2\pi}{n}$, $\mu m^{2} \cdot 2r \sin^{2} \frac{3\pi}{n}$, $\cdots$, $\mu m^{2} \cdot 2r \sin^{2} \frac{(n-1)\pi}{n}$, where $r$ is the distance of the $n$ particles from point $O$ at time $t$. Regardless of whether $n$ is odd or even, the resultant force on any particle is directed outward along the radius, and all have the same magnitude. At any time, the particles are symmetrically distributed on a circle centered at $O$ with each particle's radial vector satisfying the following equation:

$$
\begin{aligned}
& m \ddot{r} = \mu m^{2} \left[2r \sin^{2} \frac{\pi}{n} + 2r \sin^{2} \frac{2\pi}{n} + \cdots + 2r \sin^{2} \frac{(n-1)\pi}{n}\right] = \mu m^{2} nr \\
& \dot{r} \ddot{r} = \mu mn r \dot{r}, \quad \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{~d} \dot{r}^{2} = \frac{1}{2} \mu mn \mathrm{~d} r^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

By integrating both sides and using the initial condition: when $r = a$, $\dot{r} = 0$, we get

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \dot{r}^{2} = \mu mn \left(r^{2} - a^{2}\right) \\
& \dot{r} = \left[\mu mn \left(r^{2} - a^{2}\right)\right]^{1/2}
\end{aligned}
$$

For an isolated system, $E^{2}-c^{2}p^{2}$ is an invariant for all inertial reference frames, remaining constant before and after the decay. The calculations are done using the $\pi^{+}$ rest frame, which is also the center-of-mass frame.

$$
\begin{aligned}
&m_{\pi}^{2} c^{4}=\left(E_{\mu}^{\prime}+E_{\nu}^{\prime}\right)^{2}-c^{2}\left(\mathbf{p}_{\mu}^{\prime}+\mathbf{p}_{\nu}^{\prime}\right)^{2} \\
&=\left(\sqrt{c^{2} p_{\mu}^{\prime 2}+m_{\mu}^{2} c^{4}}+E_{\nu}^{\prime}\right)^{2} \\
&=2 c^{2} p_{\nu}^{\prime 2}+m_{\mu}^{2} c^{4}+2 c p_{\nu}^{\prime} \sqrt{c^{2} p_{\nu}^{\prime 2}+m_{\mu}^{2} c^{4}}
\end{aligned}
$$

When performing the above calculation, we used

$$
\mathbf{p}_{\mu}^{\prime}=-\mathbf{p}_{\nu}^{\prime}, \quad p_{\mu}^{\prime}=p_{\nu}^{\prime}, \quad E_{\nu}^{\prime}=c p_{\nu}^{\prime}
$$

From the above equation, we can solve for

$$
\begin{aligned}
p_{\nu}^{\prime} &= \frac{\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m_{\mu}^{2}\right) c}{2 m_{\pi}} \\
E_{\nu}^{\prime} &= c p_{\nu}^{\prime} = \frac{\left(m_{\pi}^{2}-m_{\mu}^{2}\right) c^{2}}{2 m_{\pi}} = \frac{\left(m_{\pi} c^{2}\right)^{2}-\left(m_{\mu} c^{2}\right)^{2}}{2 m_{\pi} c^{2}} = \frac{(140)^{2}-(106)^{2}}{2 \times 140} = 29.9 \mathrm{MeV}
\end{aligned}
$$

