problem:
An atom has four lowest energy levels:

\[ E_{0}=-14.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{1}=-9.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{2}=-7.0 \, \mathrm{eV}, \quad E_{3}=-5.5 \, \mathrm{eV} \]

The transition rate \(A_{ij}\) (Einstein coefficient) for the transition \(i \rightarrow j\) is

\[
\begin{aligned}
& A_{20} = 1.2 \times 10^{8} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}, \quad A_{21} = 8.0 \times 10^{7} \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\end{aligned}
\]

Calculate the radiative lifetime of the \(E_{2}\) energy level.

solution:
The first-order correction is
$$
\langle j m | -d E \cos \theta | j m \rangle = -d E \langle j m | \cos \theta | j m \rangle = 0
$$

Second-order correction:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \langle j+1, m | -d E \cos \theta | j m \rangle = -d E \sqrt{\frac{(j+1-m)(j+1+m)}{(2j+1)(2j+3)}} \\
& \langle j-1, m | -d E \cos \theta | j m \rangle = -d E \sqrt{\frac{(j+m)(j-m)}{(2j+1)(2j-1)}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore, the second-order correction is
$$
\begin{aligned}
E^{(2)} = & \frac{2 I d^{2} E^{2}}{\hbar^{2}} \\
& \times \left\{\frac{(j+1-m)(j+1+m)}{(2j+1)(2j+3)[j(j+1)-(j+1)(j+2)]} + \frac{(j+m)(j-m)}{(2j+1)(2j-1)[j(j+1)-j(j-1)]}\right\} \\
= & \frac{I d^{2} E^{2} \left[ j(j+1)-3m^{2} \right]}{\hbar^{2} j(j+1)(2j-1)(2j+3)}
\end{aligned}
$$

The conservation of strangeness requires the simultaneous production of a $\mathrm{K}^{+}$ when a $\mathrm{K}^{-}$ meson is produced. The conservation of the third component of isospin demands that there should also be a neutron in the final state. Therefore, the reaction equation is

$$
\pi^{-}+\mathrm{p} \rightarrow \mathrm{~K}^{-}+\mathrm{K}^{+}+\mathrm{n}
$$

Let the threshold energy and threshold momentum of the $\pi^{-}$ be $E_{\pi}$ and $p_{\pi}$, respectively. Using the mass-shell condition, the invariant mass squared of the system is

$$
S=\left(E_{\pi}+m_{\mathrm{p}}\right)^{2}-\left(p_{\pi}\right)^{2}=m_{\mathrm{p}}^{2}+m_{\pi}^{2}+2 m_{\mathrm{p}} E_{\pi}=\left(2 m_{\mathrm{K}}+m_{\mathrm{n}}\right)^{2}
$$

Thus,

$$
\begin{aligned}
E_{\pi}^{\mathrm{th}} & =\frac{\left(2 m_{\mathrm{K}}+m_{\mathrm{n}}\right)^{2}-m_{\mathrm{p}}^{2}-m_{\pi}^{2}}{2 m_{\mathrm{p}}} \\
& =\frac{(2 \times 0.494+0.939)^{2}-0.938^{2}-0.14^{2}}{2 \times 0.938}=1.502 \, \text{GeV}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let \(E\) and \(p\) be the energy and momentum of the incident proton in the laboratory frame, respectively, with \(E^{2} = p^{2}c^{2} + m^{2}c^{4}\). At the threshold of the reaction, the invariant mass squared is 

\[
(E + m_{\mathrm{p}})^{2} - p^{2}c^{2} = (m_{\pi} + m_{\mathrm{d}})^{2}
\]

The threshold energy is given by 

\[
E = \frac{(m_{\pi} + m_{\mathrm{d}})^{2} - 2m_{\mathrm{p}}^{2}}{2m_{\mathrm{p}}}
\]

The threshold kinetic energy is 

\[
\begin{aligned}
T &= E - m_{\mathrm{p}} = \frac{(m_{\pi} + m_{\mathrm{d}})^{2} - 2m_{\mathrm{p}}^{2}}{2m_{\mathrm{p}}} - m_{\mathrm{p}} \\
&= \frac{(140 + 1874)^{2} - 4 \times (938)^{2}}{2 \times 938} - 938 \approx 286.1 \ \mathrm{MeV}
\end{aligned}
\]

The wavelength for the series limit ($n \rightarrow \infty$) is given by
$$
\begin{gathered}
\frac{1}{\lambda_{\infty}}=4 R_{\mathrm{He}} \cdot \frac{1}{16} \\
\lambda_{\infty}=\frac{4 h c}{R_{\mathrm{He}} h c}=\frac{4 \times 1240}{13.6}=364.7 \, \mathrm{nm}
\end{gathered}
$$

