(1) Near the minimum of \( V(r) \), a harmonic approximation is made as \( V(r) \approx \frac{1}{2} k\left(r-r_{0}\right)^{2} \), allowing the molecule to perform harmonic oscillations around \( r_{0} \). This leads to an estimation of the vibrational energy levels. The zero-point energy of the molecule is \( E_{0}=\frac{1}{2} \hbar \omega_{0}=\frac{\hbar}{2} \sqrt{\frac{k}{\mu}} \), where \( \mu \) is the reduced mass of the vibrating system.

In comparison with HF, DF only replaces hydrogen with deuterium, retaining similar electric properties, hence they have approximately the same potential energy curve, i.e., \( k(\mathrm{DF}) \approx k(\mathrm{HF}) \), although their reduced masses are different.

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mu(\mathrm{DF}) & =\frac{m(\mathrm{D}) m(\mathrm{F})}{m(\mathrm{D})+m(\mathrm{F})} \approx 1.8 \, \text{u} \\
\mu(\mathrm{HF}) & =\frac{m(\mathrm{H}) m(\mathrm{F})}{m(\mathrm{H})+m(\mathrm{F})} \approx 0.95 \, \text{u}
\end{aligned}
\]

where u is the atomic mass unit. Thus,

\[
\frac{E_{0}(\mathrm{HF})}{E_{0}(\mathrm{DF})} \approx \sqrt{\frac{\mu(\mathrm{DF})}{\mu(\mathrm{HF})}}
\]

As a result, the zero-point energy of the DF molecule is

\[
E_{0}(\mathrm{DF}) \approx \sqrt{\frac{\mu(\mathrm{HF})}{\mu(\mathrm{DF})}} E_{0}(\mathrm{HF})=\sqrt{\frac{0.95}{1.8}} \times 0.26 \approx 0.193 \, \text{eV}
\]

$\boldsymbol{P}$ and $\boldsymbol{s}$ commute. We can discuss in the common eigenstate of $\boldsymbol{P}, s^{2}$, and $s_{z}$. The helicity $h = \frac{s \cdot P}{|P|}$ is a scalar operator:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\boldsymbol{A} = \frac{1}{2} \boldsymbol{B} \times \boldsymbol{r} = \frac{\mathrm{i}\hbar}{2} \boldsymbol{B} \times \frac{\partial}{\partial \boldsymbol{P}} \\
[h, -\frac{q}{mc} \boldsymbol{A} \cdot \boldsymbol{P}] = \frac{\mathrm{i}\hbar q}{2mc} \varepsilon_{ijs} \frac{s_{i} P_{j}}{|\boldsymbol{P}|} \cdot B_{s} \\
[h, -\frac{gq}{2mc} s \cdot \boldsymbol{B}] = -\frac{\mathrm{i}\hbar gq}{2mc} \varepsilon_{ijs} \frac{s_{i} P_{j}}{|\boldsymbol{P}|} \cdot B_{s}
\end{gathered}
$$

For the helicity to remain constant, i.e., $[h, H]=0$, $g$ must be 1.

Let $\bar{W}(t) = \frac{1}{2} W_{0}$, then we have

$$
\begin{gathered}
e^{-\frac{e^{2} \omega_{0}^{2}}{6 \pi \varepsilon_{0} m c^{3}} \tau} = \frac{1}{2}, \quad \frac{e^{2} \omega_{0}^{2}}{12 \pi \varepsilon_{0} m c^{3}} \tau = \ln 2 \\
\tau = \frac{12 \pi \varepsilon_{0} m c^{3}}{e^{2} \omega_{0}^{2}} \ln 2 = 1.1 \times 10^{-7} \, \mathrm{s}
\end{gathered}
$$

Calculate the magnetic moment of the $\mathrm{n}-\mathrm{p}$ system in the pure D state with total angular momentum $J=1$:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\boldsymbol{J} = \boldsymbol{L} + \boldsymbol{S} \\
\boldsymbol{\mu} = \frac{\left(g_{\boldsymbol{L}} \boldsymbol{L} + g_{S} \boldsymbol{S}\right) \cdot \boldsymbol{J}}{J(J+1)} \mu_{\mathrm{N}}
\end{gathered}
$$

Here, $\mu_{\mathrm{N}}$ is the nuclear magneton.\\
We need to determine $g_{\mathrm{L}}$ and $g_{\mathrm{S}}$. First, calculate $g_{S}$, in a similar manner:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\boldsymbol{S} = \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}} + \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{n}} \\
\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\mathrm{S}} = \frac{\left(g_{\mathrm{p}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}} + g_{\mathrm{n}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{n}}\right) \cdot \boldsymbol{S}}{S(S+1)} \boldsymbol{S} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = g_{S} \boldsymbol{S} \mu_{\mathrm{N}}
\end{gathered}
$$

Thus, we find

$$
g_{S} = \frac{g_{\mathrm{p}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}} \cdot \boldsymbol{S} + g_{\mathrm{n}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{n}} \cdot \boldsymbol{S}}{S(S+1)}
$$

Given $s_{\mathrm{n}} = \boldsymbol{S} - \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}}$ and $s_{\mathrm{n}}^{2} = \boldsymbol{S}^{2} + \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}}^{2} - 2 \boldsymbol{S} \cdot \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}}$, we find

$$
\boldsymbol{S} \cdot \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}} = \frac{S(S+1) + s_{\mathrm{p}}(s_{\mathrm{p}} + 1) - s_{\mathrm{n}}(s_{\mathrm{n}} + 1)}{2} = 1
$$

Similarly, $\boldsymbol{S} \cdot \mathrm{s}_{\mathrm{n}} = 1$, so we have

$$
g_{S} = \frac{g_{\mathrm{p}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{p}} \cdot \boldsymbol{S} + g_{\mathrm{n}} \boldsymbol{s}_{\mathrm{n}} \cdot \boldsymbol{S}}{S(S+1)} = \frac{g_{\mathrm{p}} + g_{\mathrm{n}}}{2}
$$

For $g_{\mathrm{L}}$, since the neutron contributes nothing to the orbital magnetic moment, the proton contributes the entire orbital magnetic moment but only half of the orbital angular momentum, i.e., $g_{\mathrm{L}} = \frac{1}{2}$. Therefore,

$$
\begin{gathered}
\boldsymbol{\mu} = \frac{\left(g_{\boldsymbol{L}} \boldsymbol{L} + \boldsymbol{g}_{\boldsymbol{S}} \boldsymbol{S}\right) \cdot \boldsymbol{J}}{J(J+1)} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = \frac{1}{2}\left[\frac{\boldsymbol{L} \cdot \boldsymbol{J} + \left(g_{\mathrm{p}} + g_{\mathrm{n}}\right)(\boldsymbol{S} \cdot \boldsymbol{J})}{J(J+1)}\right] \mu_{\mathrm{N}} \\
\boldsymbol{L} \cdot \boldsymbol{J} = \frac{J(J+1) + L(L+1) - S(S+1)}{2} = \frac{1 \times 2 + 2 \times 3 - 1 \times 2}{2} = 3
\end{gathered}
$$

$\boldsymbol{S} \cdot \boldsymbol{J} = \frac{J(J+1) + S(S+1) - L(L+1)}{2} = \frac{1 \times 2 + 1 \times 2 - 2 \times 3}{2} = -1$. Substituting back into the previous equation:

$$
\boldsymbol{\mu} = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{3}{2} - \frac{g_{\mathrm{p}} + g_{\mathrm{n}}}{2}\right) \mu_{\mathrm{N}}
$$

From the problem statement:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\mu_{\mathrm{p}} = g_{\mathrm{p}} s_{\mathrm{p}} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = \frac{1}{2} g_{\mathrm{p}} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = 2.79 \mu_{\mathrm{N}} \\
\mu_{\mathrm{n}} = g_{\mathrm{n}} s_{\mathrm{n}} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = \frac{1}{2} g_{\mathrm{n}} \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = -1.91 \mu_{\mathrm{N}}
\end{gathered}
$$

We have $g_{\mathrm{n}} = -3.82$, $g_{\mathrm{p}} = 5.58$. Substituting these into the equation gives:

$$
\mu = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{3}{2} - \frac{5.58 - 3.82}{2}\right) \mu_{\mathrm{N}} = 0.31 \mu_{\mathrm{N}}
$$

Initial state  

$$  
\psi(t=0)=\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}  
$$  

The Hamiltonian is  

$$  
H=-\mu \cdot H=\frac{|e| \hbar H}{2 m c} \sigma_{x} \equiv \hbar \omega \sigma_{x}, \quad \omega=\frac{|e| H}{2 m c}  
$$  

The equation of motion is  

$$  
\mathrm{i} \hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t} \begin{pmatrix} \psi_{1} \\ \psi_{2} \end{pmatrix}=\hbar \omega \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \psi_{1} \\ \psi_{2} \end{pmatrix}=\hbar \omega \begin{pmatrix} \psi_{2} \\ \psi_{1} \end{pmatrix}  
$$  

which implies  

$$  
\begin{aligned}  
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}  
\mathrm{i} \dot{\psi}_{1}=\omega \psi_{2} \\\mathrm{i} \dot{\psi}_{2}=\omega \psi_{1}  
\end{array}\right. \\  
& \ddot{\psi}_{1}+\omega^{2} \psi_{1}=0  
\end{aligned}  
$$  

So  

$$  
\left\{\begin{array}{l}  
\psi_{1}(t)=a \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \omega t}+b \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\omega t} \\  
\psi_{2}(t)=\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\omega} \dot{\psi}_{1}=-a \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \omega t}+b \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\omega t}  
\end{array}\right.  
$$  

With the initial conditions  

$$  
\left\{\begin{array}{l}  
\psi_{1}(0)=1 \\  
\psi_{2}(0)=0  
\end{array}\right.  
$$  

we get  

$$  
\begin{aligned}  
& \left\{\begin{array}{l}  
a+b=1 \\  
-a+b=0  
\end{array}\right. \\  
& a=b=\frac{1}{2} \\  
& \psi=\begin{pmatrix} \psi_{1} \\ \psi_{2} \end{pmatrix}=\frac{1}{2}\begin{pmatrix} \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \omega t}+\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \omega t} \\ -\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \omega t}+\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i}\omega t} \end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix} \cos \omega t \\ -\mathrm{i} \sin \omega t \end{pmatrix}=\cos \omega t\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}-\mathrm{i} \sin \omega t\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}  
\end{aligned}  
$$  

Thus, after time $\tau$, the probability of the spin being in the $-z$ direction through the Stern-Gerlach apparatus is  

$$  
\left|(0,1)\begin{pmatrix} \cos \omega \tau \\ -\mathrm{i} \sin \omega \tau \end{pmatrix}\right|^{2}=\sin ^{2} \omega \tau=\frac{1-\cos 2 \omega \tau}{2}  
$$

Using the charge neutrality equation:

\[
p + N_{D} + p_{A} = n + N_{A} + n_{D}
\]

where \(p = 0\) and \(p_{A} = 0\), the probability of electrons occupying the donor impurity energy level is:

\[
\begin{gathered}
f_{\mathrm{D}} = \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{E_{\mathrm{D}} - E_{\mathrm{F}}}{k_{\mathrm{B}} T}}} \\
n_{\mathrm{D}} = N_{\mathrm{D}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{e}^{\frac{E_{\mathrm{D}} - E_{\mathrm{F}}}{k_{\mathrm{B}} T}}} = N_{\mathrm{D}} \cdot \frac{1}{1 + \frac{1}{2} \mathrm{e}^0} = \frac{2}{3} N_{\mathrm{D}}
\end{gathered}
\]

Substituting into the charge neutrality equation, we get \(N_{\mathrm{D}} = n + N_{A} + \frac{2}{3} N_{\mathrm{D}}\), which is:

\[
\frac{1}{3} N_{\mathrm{D}} = n + N_{A} = 5 \times 10^{15} + 1 \times 10^{15} = 6 \times 10^{15} \ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}
\]

Thus, the donor impurity concentration in the doped material is:

\[
N_{\mathrm{D}} = 1.8 \times 10^{16} \ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}
\]

