Just before the collision,

\begin{equation*}
\dot{x}_{1}=\dot{x}_{2}=v_{c}=\frac{Mv}{M+2m} \tag{2}
\end{equation*}

By the conservation of mechanical energy,

$$
\frac{1}{2} M \dot{x}_{1}^{2} + 2\left[\frac{1}{2} m\left(\dot{x}_{2}^{2}+\dot{y}_{2}^{2}\right)\right] = \frac{1}{2} M v^{2}
$$

Substituting equation (2) into the above expression and solving for $\dot{y}_{2}^{2}$ yields

$$
\dot{y}_{2}^{2}=\frac{M}{M+2m} v^{2}
$$

Because

$$
y_{2}=a \sin \theta, \quad \dot{y}_{2}=a \cos \theta \dot{\theta}
$$

At collision, when $\theta=0, \dot{y}_{2}=a \dot{\theta}$, therefore

$$
a^{2} \dot{\theta}^{2} = \frac{Mv^{2}}{M+2m}, \quad \dot{\theta}^{2} = \frac{Mv^{2}}{(M+2m) a^{2}}
$$

By conservation of momentum,

\begin{equation*}
M \dot{x}_{1}+2m \dot{x}_{2}=Mv \tag{3}
\end{equation*}

And,

$$
x_{2}=x_{1}-a \cos \theta, \quad \dot{x}_{2}=\dot{x}_{1}+a \sin \theta \dot{\theta}
$$

Equation (3) becomes

$$
(M+2m) \dot{x}_{1} + 2ma \sin \theta \dot{\theta} = Mv
$$

Differentiating both sides with respect to $t$,

$$
(M+2m) \ddot{x}_{1} + 2ma \sin \theta \ddot{\theta} + 2ma \cos \theta \dot{\theta}^{2} = 0
$$

At the moment of collision, $\theta=0, \dot{\theta}^{2}=\frac{Mv^{2}}{(M+2m) a^{2}}$, substituting into the equation gives the acceleration $\ddot{x}_{1}$ at collision as

$$
\begin{gathered}
\ddot{x}_{1} = -\frac{2mMv^{2}}{(M+2m)^{2} a} \\
M \ddot{x}_{1} = -2T \\
T = -\frac{1}{2} M \ddot{x}_{1} = \frac{mM^{2}v^{2}}{(M+2m)^{2} a}
\end{gathered}
$$

