problem:
A particle with mass \( m \) undergoes forced oscillation under the combined influence of the restoring force \(-kx\), damping force \(-\gamma \dot{x}\), and a periodic driving force

\[
F(t) = 
\begin{cases} 
A \sin \omega t & 0 < t \leq \frac{\pi}{\omega} \\ 
0 & \frac{\pi}{\omega} < t \leq \frac{2\pi}{\omega} 
\end{cases}
\]

Find the first term \( a_0 \) in the Fourier series when solving the steady-state kinematic equation for \( f(t) \) using the first four terms of the series.

solution:
The mechanical energy dissipated by the damping force is equal to the absolute value of the work done by the damping force.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\left|W_{d}\right| & = \int_{0}^{T} \gamma \dot{x} \cdot \dot{x} \, \mathrm{d} t = \int_{0}^{T} \gamma \dot{x}^{2} \, \mathrm{d} t \\
& = \int_{0}^{T} \gamma [-B \Omega \sin (\Omega t - \phi)]^{2} \, \mathrm{d} t \\
& = \frac{1}{2} \gamma B^{2} \Omega^{2} T = \frac{1}{2} \gamma B^{2} \Omega \cdot 2 \pi = 0.0618 \, \text{J}
\end{aligned}
$$

The most probable radius is determined by the extremum condition of the radial probability distribution:  
\begin{equation*}   
\frac{\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{d} r} \left|u_{nl}(r)\right|^2 = 0 \tag{1}   
\end{equation*} 
When $l=n-1$, $n_{r}=0$, we have  
$$u_{0, n-1}(r)=c r^{n} e^{-Z r/a_{0}}$$  
By using the extremum condition (1), it is easy to obtain  
$$r_{n}=\frac{n^{2} a_{0}}{Z}$$  
This result is consistent with the formula for the radius of circular orbits in Bohr's quantum theory.

