(2) The periodic potential field \(V(x)\) can be written as \(V(x)=V_{0}+\Delta V(x)\). The Fourier expansion coefficient \(V_{n}\) for the periodic potential field \(V(x)\) is

\[  
\begin{aligned}  
& V_{n}=\frac{1}{a} \int_{-b}^{b} \frac{1}{2} m \omega^{2}\left(b^{2}-x^{2}\right) \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} n \frac{2 \pi}{a} x} \mathrm{~d} x \\  
& =\frac{m \omega^{2}}{2 a} \int_{-b}^{b} b^{2} \mathrm{e}^{- \mathrm{i} \frac{2 \pi}{a} x} \mathrm{~d} x-\frac{m \omega^{2}}{2 a} \int_{-b}^{b} x^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{2 \pi}{a} x} \mathrm{~d} x  
\end{aligned}  
\]

\[  
\begin{aligned}  
& =-\frac{m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{4 n \pi \mathrm{i}}\left[\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}-\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}\right]+\frac{m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{4 n \pi \mathrm{i}}\left[\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}-\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}\right]-\frac{m \omega^{2}}{2 n \pi \mathrm{i}} \int_{-b}^{b} x \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2 b} x} \mathrm{~d} x  
\end{aligned}  
\]

\[  
= \frac{m\omega^2 b}{8} \cdot \frac{2b}{-i n \pi} e^{-i \frac{n \pi}{2b} x} \bigg|_{-b}^b   
- \frac{m \omega^2}{8b} \cdot \frac{1}{-i \frac{n \pi}{2b}} x^2 e^{-i \frac{n \pi}{2b} x} \bigg|_{-b}^b   
+ \frac{m \omega^2}{4b} \cdot \frac{1}{-i \frac{n \pi}{2b}} \int_{-b}^b x e^{-i \frac{n \pi}{2b} x} \, dx  
\]

Therefore,

\[  
\begin{aligned}  
V_{n} & =-\frac{m \omega^{2}}{2 n \pi \mathrm{i}} \int_{-b}^{b} x \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2 b} x} \mathrm{~d} x \\  
& =-\frac{m \omega^{2}}{2 n \pi \mathrm{i}}\left\{\left.\left[\frac{2 b}{-\mathrm{i} n \pi} x \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{2 \pi x}{a}}\right]\right|_{-b} ^{b}+\frac{2 b}{\mathrm{i} n \pi} \int_{-b}^{b} \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n x}{2 b^{2}}} \mathrm{~d} x\right\}  
\end{aligned}  
\]

\[  
\begin{aligned}  
& =-\frac{m \omega^{2} b}{n^{2} \pi^{2}}\left[b \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}+b \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2}}\right]+\left.\frac{m \omega^{2} b}{n^{2} \pi^{2}} \cdot \frac{2 b}{-\mathrm{i} n \pi} \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{n \pi}{2 b^{2}}}\right|_{-b} ^{b} \\  
& =-\frac{2 m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{n^{2} \pi^{2}} \cos \frac{n \pi}{2}+\frac{4 m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{n^{3} \pi^{3}} \sin \frac{n \pi}{2}  
\end{aligned}  
\]

Thus, for \(n=1\), the width of the first energy gap is

\[  
E_{g 1}=2\left|V_{1}\right|=\frac{8 m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{\pi^{3}}  
\]

For \(n=2\), the width of the second energy gap is

\[  
E_{g 2}=2\left|V_{2}\right|=\frac{m \omega^{2} b^{2}}{\pi^{2}}  
\]

problem:
Consider a particle with spin 0, mass $m$, and charge $e$ confined within a spherical cavity (radius $R_{0}$). The energy of the potential well is given by

\[ 
V(r) = 
\begin{cases} 
0, & |x| = r \leq R_{0} \\ 
\infty, & |x| = r > R_{0} 
\end{cases} 
\]

Find the ground state energy of the system.

solution:
The spontaneous emission coefficient

$$
A \propto \omega^3 \left|r_{kk'}\right|^2, \quad \tau \propto A^{-1}
$$

Noting that \(\left|r_{kk'}\right| \propto \frac{1}{Z}\), we find the lifetime of the 3d state muonic atom to be

$$
\begin{aligned}
\tau & = \tau_0 \frac{\omega_0^3}{\omega^3} \cdot \frac{\left|r_{kk'}\right|_0^2}{\left|r_{kk'}\right|^2} = \tau_0 Z^2 \frac{\omega_0^3}{\omega^3} = \tau_0 Z^2 \frac{1}{Z^2 \frac{m}{m_e}} \\
& = \frac{m_e}{m} \tau_0 = \frac{0.51}{105.7} \times 1.6 \times 10^{-8} \mathrm{s} = 7.72 \times 10^{-11} \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}
$$

Let the velocity of the galaxy moving away from Earth be \(v\). Due to the Doppler effect, the following relation holds:

\[
\begin{gathered}
\nu^{\prime}=\sqrt{\frac{1-v / c}{1+v / c}} \nu \approx\left(1-\frac{v}{c}\right) \nu, \quad \nu \approx \nu^{\prime}\left(1+\frac{v}{c}\right) \\
v=c\left(\frac{\nu}{\nu^{\prime}}-1\right)=c\left(\frac{\lambda^{\prime}}{\lambda}-1\right)=c(b-1)=1.10 \times 10^{6} \, \text{m/s}
\end{gathered}
\]

Since it is critically damped, we know

\[
\beta = \omega_0 = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} = \sqrt{\frac{10000}{100}} = 10 \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}
\]

The general solution in the case of critical damping is

\[
x = (A + B t) e^{-\beta t}
\]

The solution satisfying the initial conditions: \( t = 0 \) where \( x = x_0, \, \dot{x} = \dot{x}_0 \) is

\[
x = \left[x_0 + \left(\dot{x}_0 + \beta x_0\right) t\right] e^{-\beta t}
\]

The mass reaches the equilibrium position \( x(t_1) = 0 \) when \( t = t_1 \).

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left[x_0 + \left(\dot{x}_0 + \beta x_0\right) t_1\right] e^{-\beta t_1} = 0 \\
t_1 = -\frac{x_0}{\dot{x}_0 + \beta x_0} = -\frac{0.025}{-0.30 + 10 \times 0.025} = 0.5 \, \mathrm{s}
\end{gathered}
\]

According to the Geiger-Nuttall law, the relation between the energy $E_{\alpha}$ of the $\alpha$ particle and the decay constant $\lambda$ for estimating the lifetime of ${}^{197} \mathrm{Au}$ is given by

$$
\log_{10} \lambda = C - D E_{\alpha}^{-1/2}
$$

where $E_{\alpha}$ is in MeV, and $C$ and $D$ are constants related to $Z$. For a rough estimation, the constants $C$ and $D$ can be approximately substituted using the constants for Pb, with $C \sim 52$ and $D \sim 140$:

$$
\begin{gathered}
\lambda \approx 10^{\left[52 - 140 E_{\alpha}^{-1/2}\right]} \approx 10^{-25}\,(\mathrm{s}^{-1}) \\
T_{1/2} \approx \frac{\ln 2}{\lambda} \approx 6.9 \times 10^{24} \approx 2.2 \times 10^{17} \text{ years}
\end{gathered}
$$

The longest and shortest wavelengths involved in the transition are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\tilde{\nu}_{\max }=\frac{1}{\lambda_{1}}=\frac{E_{4}-E_{1}}{h c}=R\left(\frac{5}{9}+\frac{119}{108} \alpha^{2}\right), \quad \lambda_{1}=\left(\frac{5}{9} R+\frac{119}{108} R \alpha^{2}\right)^{-1} \\
\tilde{\nu}_{\min }=\frac{1}{\lambda_{2}}=\frac{E_{3}-E_{2}}{h c}=R\left(\frac{5}{9}-\frac{7}{36} \alpha^{2}\right), \quad \lambda_{2}=\left(\frac{5}{9} R-\frac{7}{36} R \alpha^{2}\right)^{-1}
\end{gathered}
\]

The wavelength difference is

\[
\Delta \lambda=\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}=0.0204 \, \mathrm{nm}
\]

This can also be determined directly from the spectroscopic terms:

\[
\begin{gathered}
\Delta T_{n l}=\frac{R \alpha^{2} Z^{4}}{n^{3} l(l+1)} \\
\therefore \Delta T_{2 \mathrm{p}}=\frac{R \alpha^{2} \times 2^{4}}{2^{3} \times 1 \times 2}=R \alpha^{2} \\
\Delta T_{3 \mathrm{p}}=\frac{R \alpha^{2} \times 2^{4}}{3^{3} \times 1 \times 2}=\frac{8}{27} R \alpha^{2}
\end{gathered}
\]

Without considering fine structure: \(\tilde{\nu}=\frac{1}{\lambda}=\left(\frac{1}{2^{2}}-\frac{1}{3^{2}}\right) R Z^{2}=\frac{5}{9} R\)

\[
\Delta \lambda=\lambda_{2}-\lambda_{1}=\frac{\tilde{\nu}_{1}-\tilde{\nu}_{2}}{\tilde{\nu}^{2}}=\frac{\Delta T_{2 \mathrm{p}}+\Delta T_{3 \mathrm{p}}}{\tilde{\nu}^{2}}=\frac{21 \alpha^{2}}{5 R} \approx 0.0204 \, \mathrm{nm}
\]

Assuming boron atoms occupy substitutional positions, calculate the crystal density using the following formula:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\rho_{\mathrm{s}}^{n} & =\frac{797488 \times \frac{28.086 \times 10^{-3}}{6.022 \times 10^{23}}+2512 \times \frac{10.811 \times 10^{-3}}{6.022 \times 10^{23}}}{1 \times 10^{5} \times\left(5.4249 \times 10^{-10}\right)^{3}} \\
& =\frac{797488 \times 28.086 \times 10^{-3}+2512 \times 10.811 \times 10^{-3}}{1 \times 10^{5} \times\left(5.4249 \times 10^{-10}\right)^{3} \times 6.022 \times 10^{23}} \\
& =\frac{22398.24797+27.157}{159.6523116 \times 6.022 \times 10^{-2}} = 2.33251 \times 10^{3} \mathrm{~kg} \cdot \mathrm{m}^{-3}
\end{aligned}
\]

During K-capture, neglecting the recoil kinetic energy, the neutrino energy is:

$$
\begin{gathered}
E_{\nu} = E_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{K}) \\
E_{\mathrm{d}}\left(\beta^{+}\right) = \left[M(Z, A) - M(Z-1, A) - 2 m_{\mathrm{e}}\right] c^{2} \\
E_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{K}) = [M(Z, A) - M(Z-1, A)] c^{2} - \varepsilon_{i}
\end{gathered}
$$

Ignoring the binding energy $\varepsilon_{i}$ of the electron in the atom, we have:

$$
\begin{gathered}
E_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{K}) = E_{\mathrm{d}}\left(\beta^{+}\right) + 2 m_{\mathrm{e}} c^{2} \\
E_{\nu} = E_{\mathrm{d}}(\mathrm{K}) = 654 \mathrm{keV} + 2 \times 511 \mathrm{keV} = 1676 \mathrm{keV}
\end{gathered}
$$

Given $P_{1} // P_{2}$. Thus, the plane of polarization of light must be rotated by $90^{\circ}$ to extinguish the light.

$$
\begin{gathered}
\varphi_{1} = \alpha d_{1} \\
\varphi_{2} = \alpha d_{2} \\
\varphi_{1} / \varphi_{2} = d_{1} / d_{2} \\
d_{2} = d_{1} \cdot \frac{\varphi_{2}}{\varphi_{1}} = 1 \times \frac{90}{20} = 4.5 \, \text{mm}
\end{gathered}
$$

Solution (1)

$$
\begin{gathered}
f = \frac{k}{r^{2}}, \quad V = \frac{k}{r}, \quad h = b v_{0}, \\
U(r) = V(r) + \frac{m h^{2}}{2 r^{2}} = \frac{k}{r} + \frac{m b^{2} v_{0}^{2}}{2 r^{2}}
\end{gathered}
$$

The equation satisfied by $r_{\text{min}}$ is

$$
\begin{gathered}
U\left(r_{\min}\right) = E_{0} = \frac{1}{2} m v_{0}^{2} \\
\frac{k}{r_{\min}} + \frac{m b^{2} v_{0}^{2}}{2 r_{\min}^{2}} = \frac{1}{2} m v_{0}^{2} \\
r_{\min} = \frac{1}{m v_{0}^{2}}\left(k + \sqrt{k^{2} + m^{2} b^{2} v_{0}^{4}}\right)
\end{gathered}
$$

The other negative root of $r_{\min}$ has no physical significance and is discarded.

Let the edge length of the cube be $a$ and the mass be $m$. The diagonal passes through the center of mass, and the inertia ellipsoid with respect to the center of mass is spherical, meaning the moment of inertia is the same for any axis passing through the center of mass. Let this moment of inertia be $I_{\mathrm{C}}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{\mathrm{C}} & =\int\left(x^{2}+y^{2}\right) \mathrm{d} m=\int_{0}^{a} \mathrm{~d} z \int_{0}^{a} \mathrm{~d} x \int_{0}^{a}\left(x^{2}+y^{2}\right) \rho \mathrm{d} y \\
& =\rho \int_{0}^{a} \mathrm{~d} z \int_{0}^{a} \mathrm{~d} x \int_{0}^{a}\left(x^{2}+y^{2}\right) \mathrm{d} y=\frac{1}{6} \rho a^{5}=\frac{1}{6} ma^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

For symmetric decay, the conservation of energy and momentum requires:

$$
\begin{aligned}
E_{\mathrm{J}} &= E_{e^{+}} + E_{e^{-}} = 2E_{\mathrm{e}} \\
E_{\mathrm{e}} &= \frac{1}{2}E_{\mathrm{J}} = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{p_{\mathrm{J}}^{2} + m_{\mathrm{J}}^{2}} = \frac{1}{2} \sqrt{100^{2} + 3.097^{2}} = 50.024 \, \text{GeV}
\end{aligned}
$$

problem:
Find the chemical potential $\mu_0$ of a strongly degenerate Fermi gas at a temperature $T \approx 0 \, \mathrm{K}$. The density of states for the gas is given by

\begin{equation*}
D(\varepsilon) = a \sqrt{\varepsilon}
\end{equation*}

where $a = \frac{2 \pi g V (2 m)^{3/2}}{h^3}$ and $g$ is the degeneracy factor.

solution:
Let $\mu_0 = \mu(T = 0 \, \mathrm{K})$. The chemical potential can be determined by the integration:

\begin{equation*}
N = \int_{0}^{\mu_0} D(\varepsilon) \, \mathrm{d} \varepsilon = \frac{2}{3} a \mu_0^{3/2}
\end{equation*}

Therefore, the chemical potential $\mu_0$ is

\begin{equation*}
\mu_0 = \left(\frac{3 N}{2 a}\right)^{2/3}
\end{equation*}

According to the conservation of momentum, the momentum of the composite particle is

$$
\begin{aligned}
& p_{M}=p=\sqrt{\left(3 m_{0}\right)^{2}-m_{0}^{2}}=\sqrt{8} m_{0} \\
& \quad E_{M}=\sqrt{p_{M}^{2}+M^{2}}=\sqrt{17+8} m_{0}=5 m_{0} \\
& \beta=\frac{p_{M}}{E_{M}}=\frac{2}{5} \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

The velocity of the particle is

$$
v=\beta c=1.7 \times 10^{10} \, \text{(cm/s)}
$$

$$
\begin{gathered}
m \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t} + v_{r} \frac{\mathrm{d} m}{\mathrm{d} t} = 0, \quad \frac{\mathrm{d} v}{\mathrm{d} t} = a \\
\int_{m_{0}}^{\frac{1}{k} m_{0}} \frac{\mathrm{d} m}{m} = -\int_{0}^{t} \frac{a}{v_{r}} \mathrm{d} t \\
\ln \frac{1}{k} = -\frac{a}{v_{r}} t \\
t = \frac{1}{a} v_{r} \ln k \\
s = \frac{1}{2} a t^{2} = \frac{1}{2 a} \left(v_{r} \ln k\right)^{2}
\end{gathered}
$$

For solar radiation in the microwave band at a wavelength of $\lambda = 3 \, \mathrm{cm}$, $x = h \nu / k T \ll 1$, hence $\exp(x) \approx 1 + x$; equation (4) becomes

\begin{equation*}
I(\nu, T) = \frac{2 \pi k T \nu^{2}}{c^{2}} \tag{5}
\end{equation*}

From equation (5), the microwave power emitted by the Sun in the microwave band at a wavelength of $\lambda = 3 \, \mathrm{cm}$ per megahertz bandwidth is

\begin{equation*}
4 \pi R_{\mathrm{s}}^{2} \cdot I(\nu, T) \Delta \nu = 4 \pi R_{\mathrm{s}}^{2} \cdot \frac{2 \pi k T \nu^{2}}{c^{2}} \Delta \nu = 4 \pi R_{\mathrm{s}}^{2} \cdot \frac{2 \pi k T}{\lambda^{2}} \cdot \Delta \nu = 1.82 \times 10^{9} \, \mathrm{W} \tag{6}
\end{equation*}

The point at the tuning fork can be approximated as a node, and the point at the pulley is a node. The distance $l$ from the tuning fork to the pulley is an integer multiple of half the wavelength,

$$
\begin{aligned}
l &= \frac{1}{2} n \lambda, \quad \lambda = \frac{2 l}{n} \\
v &= \sqrt{\frac{T}{\eta}}, \quad v = \lambda \nu, \quad T = Mg \\
M &= \frac{T}{g} = \frac{1}{g} \eta v^{2} = \frac{1}{g} \eta (\lambda \nu)^{2} = \frac{1}{g} \eta \left(\frac{2 l}{n}\right)^{2} \nu^{2} \\
&= \frac{1}{9.8} \times \frac{0.15 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{-2}} \left(\frac{2 \cdot 1}{n}\right)^{2} (50)^{2} = \frac{15.3}{n^{2}} \ \mathrm{kg}
\end{aligned}
$$

When $n = 2$, $M = 3.8 \ \mathrm{kg}$.

If the proton is a charged spherical shell with radius \( R \), then the potential energy of the hydrogen atom is

\[
V(r) = \begin{cases}
-\frac{e^{2}}{R}, & 0 \leq r \leq R \\
-\frac{e^{2}}{r}, & R \leq r < \infty
\end{cases}
\]

When \( R \ll a_{0} \), the deviation of the above potential from the point proton Coulomb potential can be considered as a perturbation,

\[
H^{\prime} = \begin{cases}
\frac{e^{2}}{r} - \frac{e^{2}}{R}, & 0 \leq r \leq R \\
0, & R \leq r < \infty
\end{cases}
\]

The change in the ground state energy due to the perturbation is

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta E & = \int_{0}^{R} \mathrm{d}r \left(\frac{e^{2}}{r} - \frac{e^{2}}{R}\right) r^{2} R_{10}^{2} \\
& = \frac{4}{a_{0}^{3}} \int_{0}^{R} \mathrm{d}r \left(\frac{e^{2}}{r} - \frac{e^{2}}{R}\right) r^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2r/a_{0}} \\
& \approx \frac{4}{a_{0}^{3}} \int_{0}^{R} \mathrm{d}r \left(\frac{e^{2}}{r} - \frac{e^{2}}{R}\right) r^{2} = \frac{2 e^{2} R^{2}}{3 a_{0}^{3}}, \quad R \ll a_{0}
\end{aligned}
\]

In the free electron gas model, the size of the Fermi sphere is determined by the electron density in the metal, whose Fermi wave vector is

\[
k_{\mathrm{F}} = \left(3 n \pi^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{3}}
\]

Metallic potassium has a body-centered cubic structure with a volume of \(a^3\), containing 2 electrons per unit cell, therefore \(n = \frac{2}{a^3}\). Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
k_{\mathrm{F}} & = \left(3 n \pi^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{3}} = \left(3 \times \frac{2}{a^{3}} \pi^{2}\right)^{\frac{1}{3}} = \left(\frac{6\pi^{2}}{a^3}\right)^{\frac{1}{3}} \\
& = 1.240 \times \frac{3.14}{5.333 \times 10^{-10}} = 7.31 \times 10^{9} \ (\mathrm{m}^{-1})
\end{aligned}
\]

Also,

\[
E_{\mathrm{F}}^{0} = \frac{\hbar^{2} k_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}}{2 m^{*}} = k_{\mathrm{B}} T_{\mathrm{F}}
\]

Therefore,

\[
\begin{aligned}
m^{*} & = \frac{\hbar^{2} k_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}}{2 k_{\mathrm{B}} T_{\mathrm{F}}} = \frac{\left(1.055 \times 10^{-34}\right)^{2} \times \left(7.31 \times 10^{9}\right)^{2}}{2 \times 1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 1.97 \times 10^{4}} \\
& = 1.0939 \times 10^{-30} = 1.2 m_{0}
\end{aligned}
\]

where \(m_{0}\) is the rest mass of the electron.

The half-life of ${ }^{239} \mathrm{Pu}$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
T_{1 / 2} & = \frac{\ln 2}{\lambda} = \frac{N_{0} \ln 2}{(\mathrm{d} N / \mathrm{d} t)} = \frac{3.003 \times 10^{23} \times \ln 2}{2.756 \times 10^{11}} \\
& = 7.55 \times 10^{11}(\mathrm{~s}) = 2.39 \times 10^{4} \text{ years }
\end{aligned}
$$

**Solution Method 1:** When \( x < 0 \), the wave function remains in the form as in equation (1). For \( 0 < x < t \), the Schrödinger equation is

\[
-\frac{\hbar^{2}}{2m} \nabla^{2} \psi = 0
\]

If \( \psi \) takes the form: \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{k x} \), then

\[
-k^{\prime 2} + k^{2} = 0, \quad k = \pm k^{\prime}
\]

Thus, the wave function for \( 0 < x < t \) is

\[
\psi_{2}(x, y) = \left(a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x}\right) \mathrm{e}^{i k_{y} \cdot y}
\]

Overall, we have

\[
\begin{array}{ll}
x < 0, & \psi_{1}(x) = \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} + r \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} k_{x} x} \\
0 < x < t, & \psi_{2}(x) = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} x} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} x} \\
x > t, & \psi_{3}(x) = c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} x}
\end{array}
\]

Where

\[
k_{x} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \cos \theta, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

Only the \( x \) component is written here. The \( y \) component is simply \( \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{y} \cdot y} \).

\[
k_{y} = \sqrt{\frac{2mE}{\hbar^{2}}} \sin \theta
\]

All boundary conditions are

\[
\begin{gathered}
\left\{
\begin{array}{l}
1+r = a+b \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x}(1-r) = k^{\prime}(a-b) \\
c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} + b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{array}
\right. \\
\mathrm{i} k_{x} \cdot c \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} k_{x} t} = k^{\prime} a \mathrm{e}^{k^{\prime} t} - k^{\prime} b \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}
\end{gathered}
\]

Solving, we have

\[
\begin{gathered}
r = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} - 1}{1 - \beta^{2} \mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t}}, \quad \beta = \frac{k^{\prime} - \mathrm{i} k_{x}}{k^{\prime} + \mathrm{i} k_{x}} \\
|R|^{2} = |r|^{2} = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{gathered}
\]

**Solution Method 2:** The problem can also be solved by using the method of amplitude superposition, similar to the case of a Fabry-Perot interferometer in optics.

Only considering the \( x \) component, let \( T_{12}, R_{12} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 1 to medium 2 respectively. Let \( T_{21}, R_{21} \) represent the amplitude transmission and reflection coefficients for the wave from medium 2 to medium 1 respectively. Given that the incident wave amplitude is 1, the amplitude of the wave emerging from medium 2 is

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21} \cdot T_{21} + T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \cdot \left(R_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} R_{21}\right)^{2} \cdot T_{21} + \cdots \\
& = T_{12} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} T_{21} \left[1 + R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta} + \left(R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}\right)^{2} + \cdots\right] = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where \( \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \) is the attenuation coefficient of the wave traveling from one interface to the other in medium 2.

\[
\delta = k^{\prime} t, \quad k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE(1 - \cos^{2} \theta)} / \hbar = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Using the result from (1)

\[
R_{12} = \left(k_{1 x} - k_{2 x}\right) / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right), \quad T_{12} = 1 + R_{12} = 2 k_{1} / \left(k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}\right)
\]

However,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& k_{1 x} = \sqrt{2mE} \cos \theta / \hbar \\
& k_{2 x} = \mathrm{i} k^{\prime} = \mathrm{i} \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\end{aligned}
\]

So,

\[
\begin{aligned}
& T_{12} = \frac{2 k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \cos \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \cos \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta} \\
& T_{21} = \frac{2 k_{2 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{2 \sin \theta}{\cos \theta + \mathrm{i} \sin \theta} = 2 \mathrm{i} \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
R_{21} = \frac{k_{2 x} - k_{1 x}}{k_{1 x} + k_{2 x}} = \frac{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta - \cos \theta}{\mathrm{i} \sin \theta + \cos \theta} = \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta}
\]

Thus,

\[
\begin{aligned}
T & = T_{12} T_{21} \mathrm{e}^{-\delta} \frac{1}{1 - R_{21}^{2} \mathrm{e}^{-2\delta}} \\
& = \frac{4 \mathrm{i} \cos \theta \sin \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \cdot \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}} \\
& = \frac{2 \mathrm{i} \sin 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-k^{\prime} t}}{1 - \mathrm{e}^{-4 \mathrm{i} \theta} \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}
\end{aligned}
\]

Transmittance

\[
\begin{aligned}
|T|^{2} & = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{\left(1 - \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta\right)^{2} + \left(\mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \sin 4 \theta\right)^{2}} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t}}{1 + \mathrm{e}^{-4 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Reflectance

\[
\begin{aligned}
1 - |T|^{2} & = 1 - \frac{4 \sin^{2} 2 \theta}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta} \\
& = \frac{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2}{\mathrm{e}^{2 k^{\prime} t} + \mathrm{e}^{-2 k^{\prime} t} - 2 \cos 4 \theta}
\end{aligned}
\]

Where

\[
k^{\prime} = \sqrt{2mE} \sin \theta / \hbar
\]

Let subscripts 1 and 2 denote helium gas (He) and nitrogen gas ($\mathrm{N}_{2}$), respectively. Initially, 0.1 mol of He gas and 0.2 mol of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas together occupy a volume of $V_{i}=4 \mathrm{~L}$, so their initial partial pressures are:

$$
p_{1 i}=\frac{n_{1} R T_{i}}{V_{i}}, \quad p_{2 i}=\frac{n_{2} R T_{i}}{V_{i}}
$$

Substituting $n_{1}=0.1 \mathrm{~mol}$, $n_{2}=0.2 \mathrm{~mol}$, and $T_{i}=300 \mathrm{~K}$, we find:

$$
\begin{aligned}
p_{1 i} & =0.615 \mathrm{~atm} \\
p_{2 i} & =1.230 \mathrm{~atm}
\end{aligned}
$$

The initial pressure of the mixed gas is:

$$
p_{i}=p_{1 i}+p_{2 i}=1.845 \mathrm{~atm}
$$

In the critical situation, the frictional force is the maximum static friction without relative motion. Let the maximum radius at which the child does not slide off be $R$, which can be calculated using the following formula:

$$
\begin{gathered}
m R \omega^{2} = \mu m g \\
R = \frac{\mu g}{\omega^{2}} = \frac{0.4 \times 9.8}{2^{2}} = 0.98 \ \mathrm{m}
\end{gathered}
$$

Therefore, the child can sit at a distance of no more than 0.98 meters from the center of the disk without sliding off.

The change in wavelength due to Compton scattering is independent of the wavelength of the incident light. When the scattering angle $\theta=180^{\circ}$, the change in wavelength is at its maximum:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta \lambda_{\mathrm{m}} & =\frac{h}{m_{\mathrm{e}} c}\left(1-\cos 180^{\circ}\right)=\frac{2 h}{m_{\mathrm{e}} c} \\
& =\frac{2 \times 1.24 \, \text{keV} \cdot \text{nm}}{0.511 \, \text{MeV}}=0.00485 \, \text{nm}
\end{aligned}
$$

The difference in energy between the incident photon and the scattered photon, which is the maximum energy of the recoil electron, is given by:

$$
E_{\mathrm{k}}=\Delta E_{\nu}=\frac{h c}{\lambda}-\frac{h c}{\lambda^{\prime}}=\frac{h c}{\lambda} \cdot \frac{\Delta \lambda}{(\lambda+\Delta \lambda)}
$$

For visible light with $\lambda_{2}=500 \, \text{nm}$

$$
E_{\mathrm{k}_{2}}=\frac{h c}{\lambda_{2}} \cdot \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\left(\lambda_{2}+\Delta \lambda\right)} \approx \frac{1.24 \, \text{keV} \cdot \text{nm}}{500 \, \text{nm}} \cdot \frac{0.00485 \, \text{nm}}{500 \, \text{nm}}=2.4 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{eV}
$$

The calculation shows that the energy transferred to electrons by photons in the visible light range is very small and can be neglected. Therefore, for visible light, it is generally unnecessary to study their Compton scattering effects. The Compton scattering at the long-wavelength limit becomes Thomson scattering. Furthermore, at this moment, the photon's energy is so low that the bound electrons in the atom cannot be treated as stationary free electrons.

