The pressure at the heavy load is equal to the pressure at the piston as well as the pressure in the oil everywhere. Using this, we can calculate the mass of the heavy load that can be lifted:

\[
\begin{aligned}
m & = \frac{W}{g} = \frac{1}{g} \cdot \frac{F}{\frac{1}{4} \pi d^{2}} \cdot \frac{1}{4} \pi D^{2} \\
& = \frac{100}{9.8} \times \frac{(0.24)^{2}}{(0.04)^{2}} = 367 \, \text{kg}
\end{aligned}
\]

The tuning fork is approximately at a node, and the pulley is at a node. The distance $l$ from the tuning fork to the pulley is an integer multiple of half the wavelength,

$$
\begin{aligned}
l &= \frac{1}{2} n \lambda, \quad \lambda = \frac{2 l}{n} \\
v &= \sqrt{\frac{T}{\eta}}, \quad v = \lambda \nu, \quad T = Mg \\
M &= \frac{T}{g} = \frac{1}{g} \eta v^{2} = \frac{1}{g} \eta (\lambda \nu)^{2} = \frac{1}{g} \eta \left(\frac{2 l}{n}\right)^{2} \nu^{2} \\
&= \frac{1}{9.8} \times \frac{0.15 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{-2}} \left(\frac{2 \cdot 1}{n}\right)^{2} (50)^{2} = \frac{15.3}{n^{2}} \ \mathrm{kg}
\end{aligned}
$$

When $n = 3$, $M = 1.7 \ \mathrm{kg}$.

problem:
Based on the nuclear shell model, predict the spin and parity of ${ }_{5}^{11} \mathrm{B}$. The nuclear shell model describes particles moving under the influence of a common exponential potential and includes the coupling of spin and orbital angular momentum interaction $-2aSL$. For low-energy excitations of the exponential potential, it can be approximated using the energy levels of a three-dimensional harmonic oscillator $E=\hbar \omega\left(m_{1}+m_{2}+m_{3}+\frac{3}{2}\right)$. The ground state is spherically symmetric with $L=0$; the first excited state is a triply degenerate state with $L=1$. After considering the spin-orbit coupling, Table 2.9 gives the properties of the lowest energy levels; note that $\mathrm{P}_{3/2}$ has a lower energy level than $\mathrm{P}_{1/2}$. Neutrons and protons fill the corresponding energy levels independently.

Table 2.9  

\begin{center}  
\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}  
\hline  
Energy Level & Degeneracy & Parity \\  
\hline  
$\mathrm{P}_{1/2}$ & 2 & -1 \\  
$\mathrm{P}_{3/2}$ & 4 & -1 \\  
$\mathrm{S}_{1/2}$ & 2 & 1 \\  
\hline  
