\end{center}

solution:
Method 1: In the \( S \) frame, the elapsed time is \( \Delta t \), and for an observer in the \( S^{\prime} \) frame, the clock in the \( S \) frame appears as a moving clock. Due to the time dilation effect, the elapsed time should be \( \Delta t^{\prime} \sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}} \). Hence, from the observer's perspective, the clock at point \( B \) is faster than the clock at point \( A \) by an amount

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta & = \Delta t - \Delta t^{\prime} \sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}} = \Delta t^{\prime}\left[\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} - \sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}\right] \\
& = \frac{\Delta t^{\prime}\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}{\sqrt{1-\left(\frac{v}{c}\right)^{2}}} = 18 \, \text{min}
\end{aligned}
\]

Method 2: Directly use the time difference formula

\[
\delta = \frac{v}{c^{2}} \Delta x = \frac{v}{c^{2}} \Delta L = \frac{0.6c}{c^{2}} \times 5.4 \times 10^{11} = 1.06 \times 10^{3} \, \text{s} = 18 \, \text{min}
\]

The equation $\frac{1}{p} \mathrm{~d} p = -\frac{m g}{k T} \mathrm{~d} h$ remains valid. The adiabatic condition gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
& p^{\frac{1-\gamma}{\gamma}} T = \text{constant} \\
& \gamma = \frac{c_{\mathrm{P}}}{c_{v}} \approx \frac{7}{5} \text{ (for diatomic molecules)}
\end{aligned}
$$

Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{\mathrm{d} T}{T} = \frac{\gamma}{\gamma-1} \cdot -\frac{m g}{k T} \mathrm{~d} h \\
& T - T_{0} = -\frac{(\gamma-1) m g}{\gamma k}(h - h_{0}) = -\frac{(\gamma-1) M g}{\gamma R} (h - h_{0}) \\
& \frac{\mathrm{~d} T}{\mathrm{~d} h} = -\frac{\gamma-1}{\gamma} \frac{M g}{R} \approx -0.01 (\mathrm{~K} / \mathrm{m})
\end{aligned}
$$

The solution involves the fact that since the \( S^{\prime} \) frame moves along the \( x \) axis of the \( S \) frame, the projection of the meter stick in the \( y \) direction is equal to its projection in the \( y^{\prime} \) direction. This can be expressed as:

\[
\begin{aligned}
l \sin \alpha & = l^{\prime} \sin \alpha^{\prime} = 1 \cdot \sin 30^{\circ} = 0.5 \, \mathrm{m}, \\
l & = \frac{0.5}{\sin \alpha} = \frac{0.5}{\sin 45^{\circ}} \approx 0.707 \, \mathrm{m}
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore, the length of the meter stick as measured in the \( S \) frame is approximately \( 0.707 \, \mathrm{m} \).

Condition for extremum:
\[
